=begin
  Documentation: https://elanthipedia.play.net/Lich_script_repository#remedy
=end

# remedy.lic crafts one alchemy remedy (a cream, salve, unguent, elixir and so
# on) from a recipe, then leaves the product in hand and prints how many
# uses it holds ("Remedy: made 12 uses of clubmoss liniment.").
#
# == Command forms
# The book form, eight arguments: book type, chapter, recipe name, herb1,
# herb2, catalyst, vessel, product noun. Wrap a value of several words in
# double quotes. A recipe name that is not a valid Ruby pattern (an unclosed
# [ or a trailing \) stops remedy with a message before anything is sent.
# herb1 red flower or blue flower, or a part of one such as red, blue or
# flower, is crafted as dried flower or crushed flower when the character has
# none of the fresh flower. herb2 is na, none or "not used" when the
# recipe needs one herb. The vessel is the start of a word of an
# alchemy_tools entry (mort finds a large mortar; bowl finds a cauldron or a
# bowl). Lich lowercases every value.
#   ;remedy remedies 2 "blister cream" "red flower" nemoih bar mortar cream
#   ;remedy remedies 2 "blister cream" nemoih "not used" bar mortar cream
#
# The book form takes an optional ninth argument, the herb quantity: the
# fewest pieces of herb1 to put in the vessel, a whole number from 1 to 75
# (the largest stack the game makes). remedy gathers stacks until one stack
# holds at least that many, then puts the whole stack in; with fewer, or
# when it cannot count the one stack it would put in, it puts the herb away
# and stops before anything goes into the vessel. Without it
# remedy gathers 25 pieces and, short of them, says so and crafts with what
# it holds, as it always has. No caller passes it.
#   ;remedy remedies 2 "blister cream" nemoih na bar mortar cream 50
#
# The continue form resumes an unfinished remedy in the vessel in hand: it
# analyzes the product, then carries on crafting it.
#   ;remedy continue na "pewter ingot" mortar unguent
#
# The instructions form crafts a remedy from loose crafting instructions,
# "<noun> instructions", instead of a recipe book: herb1, catalyst,
# vessel, product noun and the optional herb quantity, as in the book
# form, with no herb2. remedy gets the instructions (from
# crafting_container, or alchemy_belt when it lists them), studies them
# (`study my <noun> instructions`, again when the game asks), puts them
# back, and says how many uses they have left, or that the study used
# their last use and the game tossed them. No book is opened.
#   ;remedy instructions marram "pewter ingot" mortar salve
#
# == Callers
# workorders.lic:831/833, craft.lic:694 and create_remedies.lic:54/56 run the
# book form with a base-recipes.yaml recipe. Lich gives no exit status: the
# finished remedy is left in hand, the tools and the vessel are put away,
# the product is counted (`count my <noun>`, after the mark's roundtime) and
# the script ends. The product in hand is the success signal; each caller
# checks DRCI.in_hands?(noun) after remedy and stops when it is missing.
# remedy prints a bold "Remedy:" line and exits with no product when the
# game refuses the herb ("You need a more suitable type of herb", or more
# pieces of it), when the studied instructions fade before crafting begins,
# when the game stops answering a crafting command, when the remedy is not
# done after 100 crafting commands, when it gathers fewer pieces of herb1
# than a herb quantity asks for, when a tool, the remed book, the
# catalyst or the second herb cannot be got or put in, when the crafting
# instructions of the instructions form cannot be got or studied (before
# any herb is gathered), when the game asks
# for a second herb and herb2 is na, none or "not used" (or the order is
# the instructions form, which takes none), when the finished
# remedy is not in hand after it is taken out of the vessel or after a
# stamp repair (remedy_repair_stamp), or when water, alcohol or a coal
# nugget must be bought and base-crafting.yaml knows no alchemy shop in the
# crafting town or hometown. A stop leaves the vessel
# in hand and puts the stirrer away; when the vessel holds a remedy the
# game has begun and not finished, remedy prints the ;remedy continue line
# that carries it on.
# However remedy ends, it releases the magic crafting_training_spells left
# prepared, when that setting lists a spell.
#
# == Settings read
# crafting_container, crafting_items_in_container, alchemy_belt (the tools
# and the remed book are untied from it and tied back when it lists them),
# alchemy_tools, herb_container, water_container, alcohol_container,
# master_crafting_book, mark_crafted_goods, remedy_repair_stamp,
# cube_armor_piece, crafting_training_spells, hometown, force_crafting_town
# and alchemy_room.
# The commons it calls read more (DRCA.crafting_magic_routine,
# DRCM.get_money_from_bank). remedy runs ;buff remedy, which casts
# waggle_sets['remedy'], unless waggle_sets is unset or has no remedy set.
#
# == A damaged stamp
# When mark_crafted_goods is set and the game says the stamp is too badly
# damaged to mark the remedy, remedy says so and leaves the remedy
# unmarked. With remedy_repair_stamp: true (off by default) it repairs the
# stamp with your own wire brush and oil (DRCC.repair_own_tools, as
# workorders_repair_own_tools does), then marks once more. The repair may
# walk to the bank, and to the blacksmithing finisher shop of the crafting
# town to buy oil or a wire brush, then back; the remedy waits in
# crafting_container meanwhile, and is in hand again when remedy ends.
#
# == Buying supplies
# With no water, alcohol or coal nugget catalyst on hand, remedy buys it at
# the alchemy shop of the crafting town (force_crafting_town, otherwise
# hometown; hometown's shop when base-crafting.yaml has none for the
# crafting town), with coins from that town's bank, then walks to
# alchemy_room, or back to the room it was started in when alchemy_room is
# not set.

# Crafts one alchemy remedy from a recipe book or from crafting
# instructions, or resumes one in progress.
#
# {Remedy.new} only takes the settings and the parsed arguments; {#run} does
# the work: it registers the before_dying cleanup and the Flags, finds the
# liquids, buys what is missing, then crafts until the game reports the
# remedy done.
class Remedy
  # The game's own lines can start with XML tags or follow other text; other
  # players' speech, whispers and ESP always put a double quote before their
  # text. NO_QUOTE makes a Flags matcher accept only the game's own line. bput
  # reads the text with tags stripped, so its patterns use ^ where the message
  # starts the line and ^[^"]*? where it may follow other text.
  NO_QUOTE = '^(?:<[^>]*>|[^"<])*?'

  # Every game line remedy reads, named.
  #
  # Log cites are "<log>:<line>" in the Genie client game logs of 2026-10-25
  # (25) and 2026-10-26 (26), the timestamped copy of the line. A pattern no log
  # shows is marked code-derived (unverified) and kept as a working path:
  # its absence from 84 logged runs does not prove the game never sends it.
  #
  # Each list is exactly the pattern list remedy has always given DRC.bput or
  # Flags.add; a String it used to pass is written as the Regexp bput turns
  # it into (/<string>/i). The text bput hands back is compared where it is
  # used.
  #
  # @api private
  module Patterns
    # The need cues: the game asks for an ingredient before the next step.

    # "You need another splash of water to continue crafting some unfinished
    # nemoih unguent. ..." (25:50210).
    NEED_WATER = [/#{NO_QUOTE}You need another splash of water/i].freeze

    # "You need another splash of alcohol to continue crafting an unfinished
    # clubmoss liniment. ..." (25:10873, 26:5918).
    NEED_ALCOHOL = [/#{NO_QUOTE}You need another splash of alcohol/i].freeze

    # "You need another catalyst material to continue crafting an unfinished
    # clubmoss liniment. ..." (25:11049, 26:39150).
    NEED_CATALYST = [/#{NO_QUOTE}You need another catalyst material/i].freeze

    # The cue for the second herb.
    #
    # @note code-derived (unverified): no log shows it (both logged
    #   characters made one-herb recipes); kept for two-herb recipes.
    NEED_HERB = [/#{NO_QUOTE}You need another prepared herb/i].freeze

    # The step cues: the game asks for a step with a tool or the vessel.

    # "Clumps of material stick to the sides of the container. ..."
    # (26:7707) and "Once finished you notice clumps of material forming on
    # the edge of the cauldron." (26:6031): turn the vessel.
    NEED_TURN = [/#{NO_QUOTE}Clumps of material stick to the sides/i,
                 /#{NO_QUOTE}Once finished you notice clumps of material/i].freeze

    # "As you finish, the mixture begins to transition colors." (25:11004)
    # and "... you should smell the mixture to identify what might be wrong."
    # (26:12950): smell the product. "smell the mixture" also covers the
    # "you should smell the mixture" pattern remedy used to list as well, so
    # the set of lines that set the Flag is the same.
    NEED_SMELL = [/#{NO_QUOTE}begins to transition colors/i,
                  /#{NO_QUOTE}smell the mixture/i].freeze

    # "Upon completion you see some particulate clouding up the mixture."
    # (25:11149) and "... you should push the liniment with a sieve to skim
    # off the material." (25:11959): push the product with the sieve.
    NEED_SIEVE = [/#{NO_QUOTE}Upon completion you see some particulate clouding up the mixture./i,
                  /#{NO_QUOTE}with a sieve/i].freeze

    # Replies to `count <liquid> in my <vessel>`. "I could not find what you
    # were referring to." (25:13762) means the vessel holds none of that
    # liquid; anything else, a timeout included, means it does.
    #
    # @note code-derived (unverified): "parts left of the" / "part left of
    #   the" appear in no log (neither logged character set a vessel).
    VESSEL_COUNT_REPLIES = [/^[^"]*?parts left of the/i,
                            /^[^"]*?part left of the/i,
                            /^I could not find what you were referring to./i].freeze

    # The text bput returns when the last of {VESSEL_COUNT_REPLIES} matches
    # the real line (25:13762).
    NO_SUCH_ITEM_REPLY = 'I could not find what you were referring to.'.freeze

    # Replies to `touch my <cube armor piece>`.
    #
    # @note code-derived (unverified): no log shows a cube touch.
    CUBE_TOUCH_REPLIES = [/^Warm vapor swirls around your head in a misty halo/,
                          /^A thin cloud of vapor manifests with no particular effect./,
                          /^Touch what/].freeze

    # The reply to `count my <herb>`: "You count out 75 pieces of material
    # there." (25:8678), 25 (25:33679), 21 (25:12538).
    HERB_COUNT_REPLIES = [/^You count out \d+/i].freeze

    # The reply to `count my first <herb>` (25:8678's wording).
    FIRST_STACK_COUNT_REPLIES = [/^You count out \d+ pieces/i].freeze

    # Replies to `count my second <herb>`: the count (25:8678's wording) or
    # "I could not find what you were referring to." (25:13762) when there
    # is no second stack.
    SECOND_STACK_COUNT_REPLIES = [/^I could not find/i, /^You count out \d+ pieces/i].freeze

    # Replies to `combine my <herb>`: "You combine the stacks of herbs
    # together." (25:11425), "That stack of herbs is too large to add more
    # to." (26:32077) and "You must be holding both substances to combine
    # them. ..." (25:18266, 25:104590).
    COMBINE_REPLIES = [/^[^"]*?You combine/i, /^[^"]*?too large to add/i, /^You must be holding both/i].freeze

    # Replies to the herb put ({HerbGatherer}: `put #<id> in my <vessel>`,
    # or `put <herb> in my <vessel>` when the stack in hand has no id): "You
    # put your nemoih in your large mortar." (26:10749, the reply to
    # 26:10748 `put my nemoih in my large mortar`).
    #
    # @note code-derived: the reply to the id form is taken to be the same
    #   "You put your <herb> in your <vessel>."; no log sends the id form.
    #   The id form also listens for {NOT_FOUND}, and on it the put is sent
    #   again by name ({HerbGatherer#put_herb}); the name form listens for
    #   these replies alone, as it always has.
    # @note code-derived (unverified): "already has something", "can only
    #   hold" and "You realize the" appear in no log. "You vigorously rub" is
    #   the catalyst reply (25:11081), never seen for a herb put.
    HERB_PUT_REPLIES = [/^You put/i,
                        /^[^"]*?already has something/i,
                        /^[^"]*?can only hold/i,
                        /^You realize the/i,
                        /^[^"]*?You vigorously rub/i].freeze

    # The reply to the first `tilt my <vessel>`, emptying a vessel that
    # already holds something.
    #
    # @note code-derived (unverified): no log shows the vessel tilted (the
    #   one logged "You grab" line, 25:22942, drags a body).
    TILT_REPLIES = [/You grab/i].freeze

    # Replies to the second `tilt my <vessel>`.
    #
    # @note code-derived (unverified), as {TILT_REPLIES}.
    TILT_AGAIN_REPLIES = [/You grab/i, /Roundtime/i].freeze

    # Replies to the herb put after a tilt (26:10749's wording).
    #
    # @note code-derived (unverified): "can only hold" appears in no log.
    HERB_PUT_AGAIN_REPLIES = [/You put/i, /can only hold/i].freeze

    # Replies to a crafting command (`crush ...`, `mix ...`, `analyze ...`):
    # - "Applying the final touches, you complete working on a belradi
    #   elixir." (26:6742);
    # - "Interesting thought really... but no." (25:11264) and "Try as you
    #   might, you just can't mix the tonic." (25:58002), which follow a
    #   completed remedy in every logged case;
    # - "That craftable requires more pieces of that plant to begin",
    #   code-derived (unverified), in no log;
    # - "You need a more suitable type of herb if you want to craft that."
    #   (25:50933), the whole reply to `crush my georin with my pestle`
    #   (25:50932): the game refuses the herb;
    # - "Roundtime: 7 sec." (25:10825): the step is done, craft on.
    CRAFT_REPLIES = [/^Applying the final touches/i,
                     /^[^"]*?Interesting thought really... but no./i,
                     /^[^"]*?you just can't mix/i,
                     /^[^"]*?Try as you might/i,
                     /^[^"]*?That craftable requires more pieces of that plant to begin/i,
                     /^[^"]*?You need a more suitable type of herb/i,
                     /^[^"]*?Roundtime:/i].freeze

    # "Pour what?" (26:22405): the game finds nothing to pour. It answered
    # `pour part alcohol in my heavy cauldron` (26:22403) with an empty
    # moonshine jug in hand (got at 26:22400), the jug rubbed only after it
    # (26:22410). The pattern has no "?": bput hands back the matched text,
    # {POUR_WHAT_REPLY}.
    #
    # @note The reply to remedy's own `pour <vessel> in my <vessel>` from an
    #   empty vessel is in no log; 26:22405 is the closest real line.
    POUR_WHAT = [/^Pour what/i].freeze

    # The text bput returns when {POUR_WHAT} matches the real line
    # (26:22405).
    POUR_WHAT_REPLY = 'Pour what'.freeze

    # Replies to adding water or alcohol: "You toss the alcohol into the
    # mortar and mix it in thoroughly." (25:10905), Roundtime, or
    # {POUR_WHAT} when the vessel poured from is empty (26:22405).
    #
    # @note code-derived (unverified): "You cannot find a way to add that as
    #   an ingredient" appears in no log.
    LIQUID_ADD_REPLIES = [/^[^"]*?Roundtime/i,
                          /^[^"]*?You toss/i,
                          /^[^"]*?You cannot find a way to add that as an ingredient/i,
                          *POUR_WHAT].freeze

    # "What were you referring to?": the game finds no such item. It
    # answered `get my pewter ingot from my botanist's toolbag` (26:39160 ->
    # 26:39162) and `put my pewter ingot in my heavy cauldron` (26:39192 ->
    # 26:39194) when the character had no pewter ingot. The pattern has no
    # "?": bput hands back the matched text, {NOT_FOUND_REPLY}.
    NOT_FOUND = [/^What were you referring to/i].freeze

    # The text bput returns when {NOT_FOUND} matches the real line
    # (26:39194).
    NOT_FOUND_REPLY = 'What were you referring to'.freeze

    # Replies to `put my <catalyst or herb2> in my <vessel>`: "You vigorously
    # rub the ingot alongside the mortar to scrape some shavings into the
    # mixture." (25:11081), Roundtime, or {NOT_FOUND} when no such item is
    # in hand (26:39194).
    INGREDIENT_PUT_REPLIES = [/Roundtime/i, /You vigorously/i, *NOT_FOUND].freeze

    # The game's refusal of a step it does not need now: "The sieve doesn't
    # appear suitable for working on some unfinished yelith tonic."
    # (26:24873) and "The cauldron doesn't appear suitable for working on
    # some unfinished yelith tonic." (26:25113). No Roundtime follows it.
    STEP_UNSUITABLE = [/^[^"]*?doesn't appear suitable for working on/i].freeze

    # Replies to a step (push with the sieve, turn, smell):
    # - "Roundtime: 8 sec." (26:10935): the step is done;
    # - {STEP_UNSUITABLE}: the game refused the step;
    # - a need cue alone, the game's refusal of a step while it waits for an
    #   ingredient: "You need another catalyst material ..." (26:39150), the
    #   whole reply to `push my curative with my turtle-shaped sieve`
    #   (26:39148). Its Flag is set as the line arrives, so the next pass adds
    #   the ingredient.
    # In the logs a need cue answers a push, turn or smell 50 times alone and
    # 22 times after the step's Roundtime line, never before it, so bput
    # still returns on the Roundtime of a step that was done.
    STEP_REPLIES = [/Roundtime/i, *STEP_UNSUITABLE, *NEED_WATER, *NEED_ALCOHOL, *NEED_CATALYST, *NEED_HERB].freeze

    # "You struggle to recall the crafting instructions you last studied as
    # they fade from your mind." (25:37919, 25:51002). It answers no command:
    # the game sends it three minutes after a study when crafting has not
    # begun (25:37919 at 12:49:16 after the study 25:37860 at 12:46:16;
    # 25:51002 at 02:33:06 after the study 25:50890 at 02:30:06).
    #
    # @note What the game answers to a crafting command once the
    #   instructions have faded is in no log: in both logged cases no
    #   crafting command followed the fade. Nor does any log show whether a
    #   new study cancels the fade of an earlier one. Both are open game
    #   checks ({CraftLoop#run}).
    INSTRUCTIONS_FADED = [/#{NO_QUOTE}You struggle to recall the crafting instructions/i].freeze

    # "Applying the final touches, you complete working on a belradi
    # elixir." (26:6742): the remedy is done. The game sends it after the
    # Roundtime line of the command that finished the remedy, in the same
    # second (26:6740 then 26:6742, both 07:25:08; 26:12371 then 26:12373
    # after a smell; 25:94847 then 25:94849), so a bput that returns on
    # Roundtime never reads it: a Flag catches it ({DONE_FLAG}). In the
    # logs 80 of the 81 final-touches lines follow a Roundtime line; the
    # other one, 26:45927, ends a pewter ingot, not a remedy.
    FINAL_TOUCHES = [/#{NO_QUOTE}Applying the final touches/i].freeze

    # The reply to `get my <noun> from my <vessel>` that hands the finished
    # remedy over: "You get some nemoih unguent from inside a diamondique
    # large mortar carved with delicate flowers which is on your grass
    # toolstrap." (26:11389) and "You get a belradi elixir from inside a
    # heavy cauldron with twisted Dwarven iron handles which is on your grass
    # toolstrap." (26:6794). Read from the first line fput returns, so it
    # starts the line; another player's speech or ESP starts with a name.
    PRODUCT_TAKEN = [/^You get .+ from inside /].freeze

    # Replies to `release spell`: "You let your concentration lapse and feel
    # the spell's energies dissipate." (25:52773), "You aren't preparing a
    # spell." (26:92609).
    RELEASE_SPELL_REPLIES = [/You let your concentration lapse/i, /You aren't preparing a spell/i].freeze

    # Replies to `release mana`: "You aren't harnessing any mana." (25:1316).
    #
    # @note code-derived (unverified): "You release all" appears in no log.
    RELEASE_MANA_REPLIES = [/You release all/i, /You aren't harnessing any mana/i].freeze

    # Replies to `release symb`.
    #
    # @note code-derived (unverified): none of them appears in the logs.
    RELEASE_SYMBIOSIS_REPLIES = [/But you haven't/i, /You release/i, /Repeat this command/i].freeze

    # The reply to `mark my <noun> with my stamp` that says the stamp must
    # be repaired before it marks anything: "too badly damaged".
    #
    # @note code-derived (unverified): no log has it. remedy has always
    #   waited for it (remedy.lic:423 at 03f11b54), and forge.lic:292,
    #   smith.lic:357, shape.lic:267, tinker.lic:301 and carve.lic:214 wait
    #   for the same text after the same command.
    STAMP_DAMAGED = /too badly damaged/i

    # Replies to `mark my <noun> with my stamp`: "You hold the liniment
    # steady and score the surface with the stamp." (25:11294).
    #
    # @note code-derived (unverified): "carefully hammer the stamp", "You
    #   cannot figure out how to do that" and "too badly damaged"
    #   ({STAMP_DAMAGED}) appear in no log (forge.lic and smith.lic read the
    #   same stamp lines).
    MARK_REPLIES = [/carefully hammer the stamp/i,
                    /You cannot figure out how to do that/i,
                    STAMP_DAMAGED,
                    /score the surface with the stamp/].freeze

    # Replies to `count my <noun>` with the finished remedy in hand: "You
    # count out 12 uses remaining." (25:11333, a clubmoss liniment), 18 for
    # an elixir (26:6924) and 5 for an unguent (26:11468); or "I could not
    # find what you were referring to." (25:13762, the game's reply to a
    # count of an item it cannot find).
    #
    # @note code-derived (unverified): the singular "use remaining" is in no
    #   log; all 84 timestamped product counts in the logs say "uses".
    #   The "I could not find" reply is real (25:13762) but answered a count
    #   of a liquid, not of a remedy.
    PRODUCT_COUNT_REPLIES = [/^You count out \d+ uses? remaining/i, /^I could not find/i].freeze
  end

  # Every Flag remedy registers, name => patterns, in the order the crafting
  # loop handles them: an ingredient the game asks for comes before a step,
  # because the game wants the ingredient first (at 25:33023-33042 it refused
  # a smell and asked for the alcohol again). Flags.add, the crafting loop
  # and {Remedy.delete_flags} all read this one list.
  FLAGS = {
    'remedy-water'    => Patterns::NEED_WATER,
    'remedy-alcohol'  => Patterns::NEED_ALCOHOL,
    'remedy-catalyst' => Patterns::NEED_CATALYST,
    'remedy-herb'     => Patterns::NEED_HERB,
    'remedy-sieve'    => Patterns::NEED_SIEVE,
    'remedy-turn'     => Patterns::NEED_TURN,
    'remedy-smell'    => Patterns::NEED_SMELL
  }.freeze

  # The Flag {Patterns::INSTRUCTIONS_FADED} sets. It asks for no step, so it
  # is not in {FLAGS}: the crafting loop reads it only before crafting has
  # begun ({CraftLoop#run}).
  FADED_FLAG = 'remedy-forgot'

  # The Flag {Patterns::FINAL_TOUCHES} sets: the remedy is done. It asks for
  # no step, so it is not in {FLAGS}: the crafting loop reads it at the top
  # of every pass ({CraftLoop#run}), so a remedy finished by a crafting
  # command or by a handler's step (a smell, 26:12371-12373) is taken out
  # without another crafting command.
  DONE_FLAG = 'remedy-done'

  # Every Flag remedy registers and deletes: the cues of {FLAGS}, in their
  # order, then {FADED_FLAG} and {DONE_FLAG}.
  REGISTERED_FLAGS = FLAGS.merge(FADED_FLAG => Patterns::INSTRUCTIONS_FADED,
                                 DONE_FLAG  => Patterns::FINAL_TOUCHES).freeze

  # Product nouns the help lists. ArgParser tries a definition's regex before
  # its options, so any word is accepted as the noun.
  NOUN_OPTIONS = %w[cream salve wash balm unguent potion tonic ointment elixir poultices draught].freeze

  # The herb2 argument, shared by both forms.
  HERB2_ARG = { name: 'herb2', regex: /\w+/i, variable: true, description: 'Prepared herb, wrap in double quotes if this is multiple words. Put none for none.' }.freeze

  # The catalyst argument, shared by both forms.
  CATALYST_ARG = { name: 'catalyst', regex: /\w+/i, variable: true, description: 'Type of catalyst.' }.freeze

  # The vessel argument, shared by both forms.
  CONTAINER_ARG = { name: 'container', regex: /\w+/i, variable: true, description: 'Type of container for creating.' }.freeze

  # The product noun argument, shared by both forms.
  NOUN_ARG = { name: 'noun', regex: /\w+/i, variable: true, options: NOUN_OPTIONS, description: 'End product' }.freeze

  # The book form's optional herb quantity: the fewest pieces of herb1 to
  # put in the vessel ({Order#herb_quantity}). ArgParser matches an optional
  # definition against the arguments left once the eight required ones are
  # taken in order (lich-5 arg_parser.rb, check_match), so it is only ever a
  # ninth argument, and every eight-argument call matches as before. The
  # pattern takes any whole number; {Order.from_args} stops on one outside
  # 1 to 75.
  HERB_QUANTITY_ARG = {
    name: 'herb_quantity', display: 'herb quantity', regex: /\A\d+\z/, optional: true,
    description: 'Fewest pieces of herb1 to put in, 1 to 75; remedy stops before the put with fewer. Without it, 25, crafting with what is found.'
  }.freeze

  # The recipe names the book form accepts: letters, whitespace, hyphens and
  # the six characters ASCII sits between Z and a ([ \ ] ^ _ and the
  # backtick), as remedy always has (/^[A-z\s\-]+$/i, spelled out here).
  # DRCC.find_recipe2 puts the name into a Regexp unescaped (lich-5
  # common-crafting.rb:449), so a closed [c] class or a \s escape still finds
  # its page. A name that is no valid Regexp is stopped by {Order.from_args}
  # (H11).
  RECIPE_NAME = /^[A-Za-z\[\\\]^_`\s-]+$/i

  # The herb1 argument, shared by the book and instructions forms: the herb
  # gathered into the vessel.
  HERB1_ARG = { name: 'herb1', regex: /\w+/i, variable: true, description: 'Prepared herb, wrap in double quotes if this is multiple words..' }.freeze

  # The book form: `;remedy <book type> <chapter> <recipe name> <herb1>
  # <herb2> <catalyst> <container> <noun> [herb quantity]`.
  BOOK_FORM = [
    { name: 'book_type', display: 'book type', options: %w[remedies].freeze, description: 'What alchemy type is this item.' }.freeze,
    { name: 'chapter', regex: /\d+/i, variable: true, description: 'Chapter containing the item.' }.freeze,
    { name: 'recipe_name', display: 'recipe name', regex: RECIPE_NAME, variable: true, description: 'Name of the recipe, wrap in double quotes if this is multiple words.' }.freeze,
    HERB1_ARG,
    HERB2_ARG,
    CATALYST_ARG,
    CONTAINER_ARG,
    NOUN_ARG,
    HERB_QUANTITY_ARG
  ].freeze

  # The continue form: `;remedy continue <herb2> <catalyst> <container>
  # <noun>`. It takes no herb quantity: the herb is already in the vessel.
  CONTINUE_FORM = [
    { name: 'continue', regex: /continue/i, variable: true }.freeze,
    HERB2_ARG,
    CATALYST_ARG,
    CONTAINER_ARG,
    NOUN_ARG
  ].freeze

  # The instructions form: `;remedy instructions <herb1> <catalyst>
  # <container> <noun> [herb quantity]`, for a remedy made from loose
  # crafting instructions, "<noun> instructions" ({Order#instructions_name}),
  # instead of a recipe book. It takes no herb2: every instructions remedy
  # of the logs uses one herb (clubmoss liniment 25:10795, marram salve
  # 25:94375, woundwort curative), so the order reads herb2 as na.
  #
  # Its first argument is the word instructions, whole: the pattern is
  # anchored, so it never matches the book form's first argument (an option
  # remedies) or the continue form's (any word holding continue), and no
  # argument list that matched a form before matches this one too (lich-5
  # ArgParser exits with its help when several forms match, lib/common/
  # arg_parser.rb:30-44 at 480bdee3). The word has no :variable or
  # :options, so the help shows it as the literal word (format_item,
  # arg_parser.rb:130-138).
  INSTRUCTIONS_FORM = [
    { name: 'instructions', regex: /\Ainstructions\z/i, description: 'Craft from crafting instructions (<noun> instructions) instead of a recipe book.' }.freeze,
    HERB1_ARG,
    CATALYST_ARG,
    CONTAINER_ARG,
    NOUN_ARG,
    HERB_QUANTITY_ARG
  ].freeze

  # The forms parse_args matches the arguments against.
  ARG_DEFINITIONS = [BOOK_FORM, CONTINUE_FORM, INSTRUCTIONS_FORM].freeze

  # The prepared flower each fresh flower is crafted with when herb1 is that
  # flower's name or a part of it (red, blue, ed flower) and the character has
  # none of the fresh one. The order matters: a part of both names (flower,
  # lower, e) takes the red flower's, as remedy always has.
  # create_remedies.lic:34-42 swaps the whole names itself before it runs
  # remedy.
  FLOWER_SUBSTITUTES = { 'red flower' => 'dried flower', 'blue flower' => 'crushed flower' }.freeze

  # The remed book, got and stowed when master_crafting_book is not set.
  REMED_BOOK = 'remed book'

  # The Alchemy rank from which studying the remed book prints
  # {BOOK_WARNING}. The warning used to be printed at exactly 175
  # (H33), so a character past 175 with no master_crafting_book was never
  # told again.
  BOOK_WARNING_RANK = 175

  # The warning printed when the remed book is studied at
  # {BOOK_WARNING_RANK} Alchemy ranks or more, worded as remedy has always
  # printed it.
  BOOK_WARNING = '*** You will need to upgrade to a journeyman or master book before 176 ranks! ***'

  # The request remedy was started with, read from parse_args' result.
  #
  # Every value is kept exactly as parse_args gives it (lowercased): herb2 is
  # whatever was typed, na, none and "not used" included, and the vessel is
  # the word the caller used, which {Tools.resolve} looks up. {#second_herb}
  # reads herb2 as the herb the game may ask for, nil when there is none.
  #
  # @!attribute [r] mode
  #   @return [Symbol] :book for the book form, :continue for the continue
  #     form, :instructions for the instructions form
  # @!attribute [r] book_type
  #   @return [String, nil] the book type (remedies); nil in the continue
  #     and instructions forms
  # @!attribute [r] chapter
  #   @return [String, nil] the chapter; nil in the continue and
  #     instructions forms
  # @!attribute [r] recipe_name
  #   @return [String, nil] the recipe name; nil in the continue and
  #     instructions forms
  # @!attribute [r] herb1
  #   @return [String, nil] the herb gathered into the vessel; nil in the
  #     continue form
  # @!attribute [r] herb2
  #   @return [String] the second herb, as given; na in the instructions
  #     form, which takes none ({INSTRUCTIONS_FORM})
  # @!attribute [r] catalyst
  #   @return [String] the catalyst
  # @!attribute [r] container_request
  #   @return [String] the vessel word the caller gave (mortar, bowl, mort ...)
  # @!attribute [r] noun
  #   @return [String] the product noun
  # @!attribute [r] herb_quantity
  #   @return [Integer, nil] the fewest pieces of herb1 to put in the vessel
  #     ({HERB_QUANTITY_ARG}), 1 to 75; nil when not given, and always in
  #     the continue form, which gathers no herb
  #
  # Defined as a constant and reopened below without a superclass: Lich
  # evals the whole script again on every run in the same session and keeps
  # its constants, and `class Order < Data.define(...)` makes a new
  # superclass each time, so a second run would raise "superclass mismatch
  # for class Order" before sending anything.
  #
  # @api private
  Order = Data.define(:mode, :book_type, :chapter, :recipe_name, :herb1, :herb2, :catalyst, :container_request, :noun, :herb_quantity)

  class Order
    # The herb2 values that say the recipe has no second herb, as parse_args
    # lowercases them: na (workorders.lic:821, craft.lic:637,
    # create_remedies.lic:53), none (the herb2 help text) and "not used"
    # (the file header's example).
    NO_SECOND_HERB = ['na', 'none', 'not used'].freeze

    # Builds an Order. herb_quantity may be left out, as in every request
    # made before it existed: nil, gather the usual {HerbGatherer::HANDFUL}.
    #
    # @param herb_quantity [Integer, nil] see {#herb_quantity}
    # @param members [Hash{Symbol => Object}] every other attribute
    def initialize(herb_quantity: nil, **members)
      super
    end

    # The herb2 an order carries when its form takes none (the instructions
    # form; the book and continue forms require herb2): na, the value every
    # caller passes for a recipe with one herb ({NO_SECOND_HERB}), so the
    # order reads, routes and prints herb2 exactly as a book-form order
    # with na does ({#second_herb}, {#herbs}, {#continue_command}).
    INSTRUCTIONS_HERB2 = NO_SECOND_HERB.first

    # Reads the request from parse_args' result, in any form of
    # {ARG_DEFINITIONS}. Only the continue form sets `continue`, only the
    # instructions form `instructions`, and only the book and instructions
    # forms a herb quantity. Stops before anything is sent when the
    # recipe name is no valid Regexp ({.recipe_name_error}) or the herb
    # quantity is one no stack can hold ({.herb_quantity_error}).
    #
    # @param args [OpenStruct] parse_args' result
    # @return [Order]
    # @raise [SystemExit] when the recipe name is no valid Regexp, or the
    #   herb quantity is not 1 to 75
    def self.from_args(args)
      error = recipe_name_error(args.recipe_name)
      Remedy.stop("the recipe name #{args.recipe_name} cannot be looked up in the book (#{error}); use letters, spaces and hyphens.") if error
      error = herb_quantity_error(args.herb_quantity)
      Remedy.stop(error) if error

      new(mode: mode_of(args),
          book_type: args.book_type,
          chapter: args.chapter,
          recipe_name: args.recipe_name,
          herb1: args.herb1,
          herb2: args.herb2 || INSTRUCTIONS_HERB2,
          catalyst: args.catalyst,
          container_request: args.container,
          noun: args.noun,
          herb_quantity: args.herb_quantity&.to_i)
    end

    # Which form of {ARG_DEFINITIONS} parse_args matched.
    #
    # @param args [OpenStruct] parse_args' result
    # @return [Symbol] :continue, :instructions or :book
    def self.mode_of(args)
      if args.continue
        :continue
      elsif args.instructions
        :instructions
      else
        :book
      end
    end

    # Why remedy cannot gather this herb quantity, or nil when it can. The
    # whole stack in hand goes into the vessel, and one stack holds at most
    # {HerbGatherer::FULL_STACK} pieces, so a quantity above that could never
    # be put in; 0 asks for no herb at all.
    #
    # @param quantity [String, nil] the herb quantity as parse_args gives it
    #   ({HERB_QUANTITY_ARG}: digits only); nil when not given
    # @return [String, nil] the sentence remedy stops with
    def self.herb_quantity_error(quantity)
      return nil if quantity.nil? || (1..HerbGatherer::FULL_STACK).cover?(quantity.to_i)

      "the herb quantity #{quantity} cannot be gathered; give 1 to #{HerbGatherer::FULL_STACK}, the most pieces one stack holds."
    end

    # Why DRCC.find_recipe2 would raise on this recipe name, or nil when it
    # would not. find_recipe2 puts the name into a Regexp unescaped (lich-5
    # common-crafting.rb:449, and DRC.bput builds /#{name}/i from its
    # pattern string), so a name such as "some [x" (an unclosed [) or one
    # ending in \ raised RegexpError there after the book was got and
    # turned (H11). Every name that compiles is left alone, as
    # remedy always has: a closed [c] class, a \s escape or a ^ is read as
    # a pattern by find_recipe2, which can still find the page.
    #
    # @param name [String, nil] the recipe name; nil in the continue form
    # @return [String, nil] the RegexpError message
    def self.recipe_name_error(name)
      return nil if name.nil?

      Regexp.new(name)
      nil
    rescue RegexpError => e
      e.message
    end

    # Whether this is the continue form, which resumes the remedy in the
    # vessel in hand.
    #
    # @return [Boolean]
    def continue?
      mode == :continue
    end

    # Whether this is the instructions form, which studies loose crafting
    # instructions ({#instructions_name}) instead of a recipe book.
    #
    # @return [Boolean]
    def instructions?
      mode == :instructions
    end

    # The crafting instructions an instructions-form order studies, as
    # `get my`, `study my` and `put my` name them: "<noun> instructions", the
    # convention forge.lic:980 uses for its instructions, and the name the
    # logs got, studied and put the remedy instructions by (`study my
    # liniment instructions`, 25:10808-10831; `study my salve
    # instructions`, 25:94392-94418).
    #
    # @return [String, nil] e.g. "salve instructions"; nil in the book and
    #   continue forms
    def instructions_name
      "#{noun} instructions" if instructions?
    end

    # The names {Inventory} gets from and stows in the herb container: herb1
    # and herb2, as given.
    #
    # herb2 stays as given here, na included: routing nil as a herb would
    # send an empty hand to herb_container ({Inventory#stow}), which remedy
    # has never done in the book form.
    #
    # @return [Array<String, nil>]
    def herbs
      [herb1, herb2]
    end

    # The second herb the game may ask for ({Patterns::NEED_HERB}), or nil
    # when herb2 says there is none ({NO_SECOND_HERB}) or is missing.
    #
    # @return [String, nil]
    def second_herb
      herb2 unless herb2.nil? || NO_SECOND_HERB.include?(herb2)
    end

    # Why the order has no second herb to add, for the stop when the game
    # asks for one anyway ({#second_herb} nil). In the book and continue
    # forms it names the herb2 typed; the instructions form takes no herb2
    # (its na is filled in, {INSTRUCTIONS_HERB2}), so it names the form
    # instead of an argument the user never typed.
    #
    # @return [String] e.g. 'herb2 is "na", which names none'
    def no_second_herb
      instructions? ? 'the instructions form takes none' : "herb2 is #{herb2.inspect}, which names none"
    end

    # The command that carries on this remedy from the vessel in hand, in
    # the continue form of {ARG_DEFINITIONS}: `;remedy continue <herb2>
    # <catalyst> <container> <noun>`, each value as given, in double quotes
    # when it holds a space (Lich splits the arguments at spaces outside
    # double quotes).
    #
    # @return [String] e.g. `;remedy continue na "pewter ingot" mortar unguent`
    def continue_command
      values = [herb2, catalyst, container_request, noun].map { |value| value.to_s.match?(/\s/) ? %("#{value}") : value.to_s }
      ";remedy continue #{values.join(' ')}"
    end
  end

  # The vessel and the stirrer a remedy is made with, and the crafting
  # commands they make. This is the one place that chooses between a mortar
  # (crush with the pestle) and any other vessel (mix with the mixing stick).
  #
  # @!attribute [r] container
  #   @return [String] the vessel, as alchemy_tools names it
  # @!attribute [r] stirrer
  #   @return [String] the pestle for a mortar, the mixing stick otherwise
  # @!attribute [r] verb
  #   @return [String] crush for a mortar, mix otherwise
  #
  # Defined as a constant and reopened below without a superclass: Lich
  # evals the whole script again on every run in the same session and keeps
  # its constants, and `class Tools < Data.define(...)` makes a new
  # superclass each time, so a second run would raise "superclass mismatch
  # for class Tools" before sending anything.
  #
  # @api private
  Tools = Data.define(:container, :stirrer, :verb)

  class Tools
    # A vessel whose name matches is a mortar.
    MORTAR = /mort/

    # The alchemy_tools entry that matches is the pestle.
    PESTLE = /pestle/

    # The pestle remedy names when alchemy_tools lists none.
    DEFAULT_PESTLE = 'pestle'

    # The stirrer for any vessel that is not a mortar.
    MIXING_STICK = 'mixing stick'

    # The crafting verb with a mortar.
    CRUSH = 'crush'

    # The crafting verb with any other vessel.
    MIX = 'mix'

    # A vessel request that matches asks for a bowl...
    BOWL_REQUEST = /bowl/

    # ... which a cauldron or a bowl in alchemy_tools answers.
    BOWL_TOOLS = /cauldron|bowl/

    # Finds the vessel and the stirrer in alchemy_tools.
    #
    # The vessel is the first alchemy_tools entry with a word that starts
    # with the requested word ({request_pattern}), so mort finds a large
    # mortar and a recipe's mortar finds a rustic mortar. A request for a
    # bowl takes a cauldron or a bowl.
    #
    # Stops when the setting lists no match, because the setting is wrong.
    # Going on with the word the recipe used sends "get my bowl", which takes
    # whichever bowl the character happens to carry, and a nil sends "get my
    # " with no item at all.
    #
    # @param settings [OpenStruct] the character's settings; reads
    #   alchemy_tools
    # @param order [Order] the request
    # @return [Tools]
    # @raise [SystemExit] when alchemy_tools lists no match
    def self.resolve(settings, order)
      container = find_container(settings.alchemy_tools, order)
      pestle = settings.alchemy_tools.find { |tool| PESTLE =~ tool } || DEFAULT_PESTLE
      if MORTAR =~ container
        new(container: container, stirrer: pestle, verb: CRUSH)
      else
        new(container: container, stirrer: MIXING_STICK, verb: MIX)
      end
    end

    # The alchemy_tools entry for the requested vessel, or a stop.
    #
    # @param tools [Array<String>, nil] alchemy_tools
    # @param order [Order] the request
    # @return [String] the alchemy_tools entry
    # @raise [SystemExit] when alchemy_tools lists no match
    def self.find_container(tools, order)
      requested = order.container_request
      pattern = request_pattern(requested)
      container = tools.to_a.find { |tool| pattern =~ tool }
      return container if container

      DRC.message("*** #{order.recipe_name} needs a #{requested}, and your alchemy_tools setting lists none. ***")
      DRC.message("*** Add your #{requested} to alchemy_tools, then run this again. ***")
      exit
    end
    private_class_method :find_container

    # The pattern an alchemy_tools entry must match to answer the request:
    # {BOWL_TOOLS} for a bowl, otherwise the requested word, escaped, at the
    # start of a word of the entry.
    #
    # The word is a prefix, with no word boundary after it, so mort still
    # finds a large mortar. It used to go into the Regexp unescaped and
    # unanchored (H12): mortar( raised RegexpError, and r took the
    # first entry holding an r anywhere (a heavy cauldron). Now a word with
    # Regexp syntax in it matches only itself, and r finds only an entry
    # with a word starting with r.
    #
    # @param requested [String] the vessel word the caller gave
    # @return [Regexp]
    def self.request_pattern(requested)
      return BOWL_TOOLS if requested =~ BOWL_REQUEST

      /\b#{Regexp.escape(requested.to_s)}/
    end
    private_class_method :request_pattern

    # Whether the vessel is a mortar.
    #
    # @return [Boolean]
    def mortar?
      MORTAR.match?(container)
    end

    # The first crafting command: a mortar crushes the herb, any other
    # vessel is mixed.
    #
    # @param herb [String] herb1, now in the vessel
    # @return [String]
    def first_command(herb)
      mortar? ? "#{verb} my #{herb} with my #{stirrer}" : "#{verb} my #{container} with my #{stirrer}"
    end

    # Every later crafting command: a mortar crushes the product, any other
    # vessel is mixed.
    #
    # @param noun [String] the product noun
    # @return [String]
    def next_command(noun)
      mortar? ? "#{verb} my #{noun} with my #{stirrer}" : "#{verb} my #{container} with my #{stirrer}"
    end
  end

  # Where remedy's things are kept, and the gets and stows that reach them.
  #
  # A herb (herb1 or herb2) comes from herb_container or the crafting
  # container when one holds it; the vessel comes from the ground after it
  # was lowered; anything else comes from wherever DRCC.get_crafting_item
  # finds it (alchemy_belt, then the crafting container). Holds no game
  # state: it only knows the settings and the herb names.
  #
  # {#stow_held}, {#stow_beside} and {#stow_vessel} put away a hand chosen
  # by the name of what it holds, giving the stow the hand's own contents,
  # as the stows by hand position always have.
  #
  # {#fetch?} and {#fetch!} are the guarded gets: they work the same on every
  # lich-5 release. {#get} and {#get_tool} are the plain gets remedy has
  # always made: the liquids, and the herb gathering, which reads the hands
  # after them ({#got?}) so its gets keep their arguments.
  #
  # @api private
  class Inventory
    # What {#get} returns when it got the item without naming a container
    # (the herb gathering reads it).
    UNROUTED = 'none'

    # How many times {#got?} looks at the hands again, a second apart, for an
    # item a guarded get did not bring at once: the 15 s DRC.bput waits for a
    # reply (BPUT_DEFAULT_TIMEOUT, lich-5 common.rb:72).
    #
    # A get whose reply comes after DRC.bput gave up still brings the item
    # (lich-5 480bdee3 common-crafting.rb:430-432: "a late reply may still
    # deliver it"). The plain get remedy used to make returned on that
    # timeout without exiting, on every release, and remedy sent its next
    # command, whose own bput waited another 15 s; the late "You get" came
    # first, so the run went on. Looking again for as long keeps that: a get
    # answered up to 15 s late still crafts on. The cost is paid only when
    # the item is not in hand, before a stop.
    #
    # A put answered late is the same the other way round: the item leaves
    # the hand after DRC.bput gave up, and the run went on. {#released?}
    # looks for as long before the herb gathering calls a leftover stuck.
    #
    # @note No get or put in the logs timed out (the one bput timeout,
    #   26:68670, is a cast), so the late reply is code-derived, not seen.
    LATE_GET_LOOKS = 15

    # How many times the herb gathering looks at the hands before a stop
    # remedy never used to make: after the first herb get ({#got?}) and
    # after the herb_container stow of the leftover ({#stow_leftover}).
    #
    # Today those gets and stows are plain: on a reply later than DRC.bput's
    # 15 s, remedy sent its next command, and the game, which answers
    # commands in order, answered the late one first, so the run went on
    # however late the reply came. A stop there must not come sooner than
    # the lag floor of three 15 s silences for the same
    # command: the command's own bput waits one, and these looks wait the
    # other two, so a reply up to 30 s after its bput gave up still goes on
    # with today's commands. The cost is paid only before such a stop.
    #
    # @note code-derived, as {LATE_GET_LOOKS}: no get or put in the logs
    #   timed out.
    STOP_LOOKS = 2 * LATE_GET_LOOKS

    # @return [String] crafting_container
    attr_reader :bag

    # @return [Array<String>, nil] crafting_items_in_container: the items
    #   DRCC.get_crafting_item gets from {#bag} by name
    attr_reader :bag_items

    # @return [Hash, nil] alchemy_belt ('name', 'items'): the tools
    #   DRCC.get_crafting_item unties and DRCC.stow_crafting_item ties back
    attr_reader :belt

    # @param settings [OpenStruct] the character's settings; reads
    #   crafting_container, crafting_items_in_container, alchemy_belt and
    #   herb_container
    # @param herbs [Array<String, nil>] the names routed as herbs
    #   ({Order#herbs})
    def initialize(settings, herbs:)
      @settings = settings
      @herbs = herbs
      @bag = settings.crafting_container
      @bag_items = settings.crafting_items_in_container
      @belt = settings.alchemy_belt
      @herb_container = settings.herb_container # normal container or special herb container both work
    end

    # The same inventory with other herb names, after herb1 is swapped.
    #
    # @param herbs [Array<String, nil>] the names routed as herbs
    # @return [Inventory]
    def with_herbs(herbs)
      Inventory.new(@settings, herbs: herbs)
    end

    # Gets an item: a herb from herb_container or the crafting container
    # when one holds it, the vessel from the ground, anything else from
    # wherever DRCC.get_crafting_item finds it.
    #
    # @param name [String, nil] the item
    # @param at_feet [Boolean] the item is the vessel, lowered to the ground
    # @param skip_exit [Boolean] ask DRCC.get_crafting_item to return
    #   instead of exiting on a missing item ({#fetch?})
    # @return [String, Object] {UNROUTED} when the item was got without
    #   naming a container, otherwise DRCC.get_crafting_item's return, which
    #   differs by lich-5 release
    # @raise [SystemExit] when DRCC.get_crafting_item stops the script
    #   (lich-5 v5.22.0 and later, on a missing item, without skip_exit)
    def get(name, at_feet: false, skip_exit: false)
      # added to check for herbs in special herb container, then check if herbs in crafting container, otherwise get normally
      # this is to prevent issues getting herbs if someone has less than 25 in the herb container
      # there are still issues when herbs are in a different container (not the bag or the herb container), script will still run fine
      if herb?(name) && @herb_container && DRCI.exists?(name, @herb_container)
        crafting_get(name, @herb_container, [name], @belt, skip_exit) # Look for herbs in herb_container
      elsif herb?(name) && DRCI.exists?(name, @bag)
        crafting_get(name, @bag, [name], @belt, skip_exit) # Look for herbs in bag
      elsif at_feet
        crafting_get(name, @bag, nil, @belt, skip_exit) # used primarily to get bowl/mortar etc. from at our feet after lowering it.
      else
        get_tool(name, skip_exit: skip_exit) # Primarily to get herb without specifying a bag
        UNROUTED # return specified to handle herbs differently for this option (to prevent a bug)
      end
    end

    # Gets an item by the routes of {#get} and tells whether it is in hand,
    # the same way on every lich-5 release.
    #
    # DRCC.get_crafting_item's answer differs by release (lich-5
    # common-crafting.rb at each tag): v5.20.0 to v5.22.0 (:324-363) return
    # nil on success too; v5.20.0 and v5.21.0 return nil without exiting on
    # a missing item, v5.22.0 exits; only v5.23.0 and later (:443-498)
    # return true or false. Every release takes skip_exit as its fifth
    # parameter. So the get passes skip_exit and the answer comes from the
    # hands ({#got?}), as forge.lic does after its book get and as the
    # helper itself does when the game says the item is not there.
    #
    # @param name [String] the item: a tool, the catalyst or a herb, never a
    #   liquid (a liquid's success comes from the reply)
    # @param at_feet [Boolean] the item is the vessel, lowered to the ground
    # @return [Boolean] whether a hand holds the item after the get, or
    #   within {LATE_GET_LOOKS} seconds of it
    # @raise [SystemExit] only when lich-5 v5.22.0 stops the script after a
    #   retry: before v5.23.0 the helper retries a get the game called too
    #   heavy, or an untie wounds hindered, without skip_exit (v5.22.0
    #   common-crafting.rb:334, :351, :360), and v5.22.0 exits when that
    #   retry finds nothing
    def fetch?(name, at_feet: false)
      get(name, at_feet: at_feet, skip_exit: true)
      got?(name)
    end

    # Whether a get brought the item: a hand holds it now, or does within
    # +looks+ seconds, looked at once a second, so a reply that came after
    # DRC.bput gave up still counts.
    #
    # @param name [String] the name the item was asked for by
    # @param looks [Integer] the looks after the first ({LATE_GET_LOOKS} by
    #   default; {STOP_LOOKS} before a stop remedy never used to make)
    # @return [Boolean] true as soon as a hand holds it ({#held?}); false
    #   after +looks+ looks find it in neither hand
    def got?(name, looks: LATE_GET_LOOKS)
      within_late_looks(looks) { held?(name) }
    end

    # Whether a put took the item out of the hands: neither hand holds it
    # now, or neither does within +looks+ seconds, looked at once a second,
    # so a put answered after DRC.bput gave up still counts.
    #
    # @param name [String] the name the item was put away by
    # @param looks [Integer] the looks after the first ({LATE_GET_LOOKS} by
    #   default; {STOP_LOOKS} before a stop remedy never used to make)
    # @return [Boolean] true as soon as no hand holds it ({#held?}); false
    #   after +looks+ looks still find it in hand
    def released?(name, looks: LATE_GET_LOOKS)
      within_late_looks(looks) { !held?(name) }
    end

    # Whether a get brought a second stack of a herb: both hands hold it
    # now, or do within +looks+ seconds, looked at once a second.
    #
    # @param name [String] the name the item was asked for by
    # @param looks [Integer] the looks after the first
    # @return [Boolean] true as soon as both hands hold it
    #   ({#in_both_hands?}); false after +looks+ looks find it in one hand
    #   or none
    def got_second?(name, looks: LATE_GET_LOOKS)
      within_late_looks(looks) { in_both_hands?(name) }
    end

    # Whether a put left one stack of a herb: at most one hand holds it
    # now, or within +looks+ seconds, looked at once a second.
    #
    # Both hands hold the herb between a combine too large and the put of
    # one stack, so {#released?}, which asks whether any hand holds it,
    # cannot tell whether that put worked.
    #
    # @param name [String] the name the item was put away by
    # @param looks [Integer] the looks after the first
    # @return [Boolean] true as soon as a hand is free of it; false after
    #   +looks+ looks still find it in both hands
    def released_one?(name, looks: LATE_GET_LOOKS)
      within_late_looks(looks) { !in_both_hands?(name) }
    end

    # Gets an item as {#fetch?} does, and stops remedy when it is not in
    # hand afterwards: "Remedy: could not get <name>. Stopping."
    #
    # @param name [String] the item, as {#fetch?}
    # @param at_feet [Boolean] the item is the vessel, lowered to the ground
    # @return [true] when the item is in hand
    # @raise [SystemExit] when it is not ({Remedy.stop}), or as {#fetch?}
    def fetch!(name, at_feet: false)
      fetch?(name, at_feet: at_feet) || Remedy.stop("could not get #{name}.")
    end

    # Whether a hand holds the item, found by the last word of its name.
    #
    # The game takes the last word of `get my <name>` as the start of the
    # item's noun, and the noun is a word of the name a hand shows, so
    # DRCI.in_hands? on that word finds every item the get can bring. The
    # whole name does not: DRCI.in_hands? wants the first word before the
    # last, and `get remed book from my botanist's toolbag` (26:5825) brings
    # "a jadeleaf-bound book of master remedies instructions" (26:5832),
    # where "remed" comes after "book".
    #
    # @param name [String] the name the item was asked for by
    # @return [Boolean]
    # @note An item of the same noun already in the other hand also counts;
    #   remedy then goes on as it always has.
    def held?(name)
      DRCI.in_hands?(noun_of(name))
    end

    # The game's id of the item a hand holds, the hand found by the last
    # word of the item's name as {#stow_held} finds it (left hand first).
    #
    # The id is GameObj's: the +exist+ attribute of the game's <left> or
    # <right> hand tag (lich-5 lib/common/xmlparser.rb:1345 and :1355,
    # GameObj.new_right_hand / new_left_hand), a String, or nil when the
    # tag carries none. GameObj.left_hand and GameObj.right_hand are nil
    # before the first hand tag (lich-5 lib/common/gameobj.rb:500-503 at
    # v5.20.0, :653-656 at v5.22.0), so they are read with a safe
    # navigation.
    #
    # @param name [String] the name the item was asked for by
    # @return [String, nil] the id, or nil when neither hand holds the item
    #   or the hand's item has no id
    def held_id(name)
      hand = hand_holding(name)
      return nil unless hand

      (hand == :left ? GameObj.left_hand : GameObj.right_hand)&.id
    end

    # What the hand holding an item shows, the hand found by the last word
    # of the item's name as {#stow_held} finds it (left hand first): the
    # full name, e.g. "nemoih unguent" for the noun "unguent".
    #
    # @param name [String] the name the item was asked for by
    # @return [String, nil] the hand's contents, or nil when neither hand
    #   holds the item
    def held_name(name)
      hand = hand_holding(name)
      hand && contents(hand)
    end

    # Puts away what the hand holding an item holds, found by the last word
    # of the item's name as {#held?} finds it. Nothing is sent when neither
    # hand holds it.
    #
    # The stow is given the hand's own contents, the full name the hand
    # shows, which is what remedy has always stowed by hand position: so
    # DRCC.stow_crafting_item ties the item to the belt, or puts it in the
    # crafting container, exactly as before. Only the
    # choice of hand changes.
    #
    # @param name [String] the name the item was asked for by (the vessel or
    #   the stirrer, as alchemy_tools names it)
    # @return [Boolean] whether a hand held the item and it was stowed
    def stow_held(name)
      hand = hand_holding(name)
      return false unless hand

      stow(contents(hand))
      true
    end

    # Puts away what the hand that does not hold the vessel holds: the
    # stirrer, or the item a crafting step used. Which hand holds the vessel
    # is found by its name ({#stow_held}'s rule); when neither hand shows it,
    # the left hand is put away, as remedy always has.
    #
    # With the vessel in the right hand, as every book-form run has it (it
    # is picked up into the empty right hand), this is the left hand, so the
    # stow is the same as before. In the continue form the player may hold
    # the vessel in the left hand: the stirrer is then in the right, and is
    # what gets put away, not the vessel with the unfinished remedy in it
    # (H14).
    #
    # @param vessel [String] the vessel, as alchemy_tools names it
    # @return [void]
    def stow_beside(vessel)
      stow(contents(hand_holding(vessel) == :left ? :right : :left))
    end

    # Puts the vessel away after the product is out: by its name when a hand
    # shows it ({#stow_held}); when neither hand does, the right hand is put
    # away, as remedy always has after the product get.
    #
    # The vessel can be in neither hand in the continue form: nothing makes
    # the player hold it, and the product get still works from a vessel tied
    # on the belt ("You get some nemoih unguent from inside a diamondique
    # large mortar carved with delicate flowers which is on your grass
    # toolstrap.", 26:11389; also 26:6794). The stirrer the crafting steps got is then in the right hand
    # (the get fills the empty right hand, so the product goes to the left),
    # and the right-hand stow puts it away as before (the
    # pre-layer-12 Finisher stowed DRC.right_hand here).
    #
    # @param vessel [String] the vessel, as alchemy_tools names it
    # @return [void]
    # @note code-derived: with the vessel in neither hand and the right hand
    #   holding the product (both hands empty at the get, e.g. no step got
    #   the stirrer), the product is put away, exactly as the right-hand stow
    #   always did. No log shows that case.
    def stow_vessel(vessel)
      stow(contents(:right)) unless stow_held(vessel)
    end

    # Puts an item away: a herb as {#stow_herb} does, anything else in the
    # crafting container or on the alchemy belt.
    #
    # @param name [String, nil] the item (a hand's contents; nil when empty)
    # @return [void]
    def stow(name)
      herb?(name) ? stow_herb(name) : stow_tool(name)
    end

    # Puts a herb stack away: in herb_container when one is set, otherwise
    # in the crafting container.
    #
    # @param name [String] the herb, as the order names herb1 or herb2
    # @return [void]
    def stow_herb(name)
      DRCC.stow_crafting_item(name, @herb_container || @bag, @belt)
    end

    # Puts away the herb left in hand after the vessel put: in
    # herb_container, then, when the hand still holds it, in the crafting
    # container.
    #
    # The hand is read because lich-5 counts "That stack of herbs is too
    # large to add more to." (26:32077), the reply of a container whose
    # stack is full, as a stow that worked (F3). It is read as
    # {#released?} does, for {STOP_LOOKS} seconds after the first stow, so a
    # stow answered up to 30 s late is not taken for a stuck herb (today's
    # one stow went on however late its reply), and the crafting container
    # is tried only when the herb stays.
    #
    # @param herb [String] herb1
    # @return [Boolean] whether the hands are free of the herb afterwards
    def stow_leftover(herb)
      stow_herb(herb)
      return true if released?(herb, looks: STOP_LOOKS)
      return false unless @herb_container

      stow_tool(herb)
      released?(herb)
    end

    # Gets an item with no herb routing: from alchemy_belt when it is one of
    # the belt's items, otherwise from the crafting container.
    #
    # @param name [String] the item
    # @param skip_exit [Boolean] return instead of exiting on a missing item
    # @return [Object] DRCC.get_crafting_item's return, which differs by
    #   lich-5 release
    # @raise [SystemExit] when DRCC.get_crafting_item stops the script
    #   (lich-5 v5.22.0 and later, on a missing item, without skip_exit)
    def get_tool(name, skip_exit: false)
      crafting_get(name, @bag, @bag_items, @belt, skip_exit)
    end

    # Puts an item away with no herb routing: on alchemy_belt when it is one
    # of the belt's items, otherwise in the crafting container.
    #
    # @param name [String, nil] the item
    # @return [void]
    def stow_tool(name)
      DRCC.stow_crafting_item(name, @bag, @belt)
    end

    # Puts a held item in the crafting container by its game id (`put
    # #<id> in my <crafting_container>`, through DRCI.put_away_item?), and
    # tells whether it left the hands, as {#released?} reads them.
    #
    # The id names the very item in hand; a name could take another item of
    # the same name.
    #
    # @param id [String] the item's game id ({#held_id})
    # @param name [String] the name the item was asked for by (its noun)
    # @return [Boolean] whether neither hand holds it afterwards, or within
    #   {LATE_GET_LOOKS} seconds
    # @note DRCI.put_away_item?'s answer is not read: the hands decide, as
    #   for every put remedy checks.
    def put_aside(id, name)
      DRCI.put_away_item?("##{id}", @bag)
      released?(name)
    end

    # Gets an item back by its game id (`get #<id>`, through
    # DRCI.get_item?, wherever it is), and tells whether a hand holds it, as
    # {#got?} reads them.
    #
    # @param id [String] the item's game id, as {#put_aside} was given it
    # @param name [String] the name the item was asked for by (its noun)
    # @return [Boolean] whether a hand holds it afterwards, or within
    #   {LATE_GET_LOOKS} seconds
    # @note DRCI.get_item?'s answer is not read: before lich-5 v5.22.0 it
    #   looks in the hands for DRC.get_noun("#<id>"), which is nil (v5.20.0
    #   common-items.rb:1249-1262), so it answers false even when the get
    #   worked.
    def take_back(id, name)
      DRCI.get_item?("##{id}")
      got?(name)
    end

    # Repairs tools with the character's own wire brush and oil
    # (DRCC.repair_own_tools, with the crafting container, its items and
    # alchemy_belt), buying what is missing at the town's finisher shop.
    #
    # @param info [Hash] the town's base-crafting.yaml blacksmithing data
    #   ('finisher-room', 'finisher-number')
    # @param tools [Array<String>] the tools to repair
    # @return [Object] DRCC.repair_own_tools' return, which remedy does not
    #   read
    # @raise [SystemExit] when the helper's get of the wire brush or the oil
    #   stops the script
    def repair_tools(info, tools)
      DRCC.repair_own_tools(info, tools, @bag, @bag_items, @belt)
    end

    # Sets the vessel down, so the herb can be gathered into it.
    #
    # @param name [String] the vessel
    # @return [Boolean] DRCI.lower_item?'s answer, which remedy does not read
    def lower(name)
      DRCI.lower_item?(name)
    end

    # Picks the lowered vessel up from the ground, guarded ({#fetch!}).
    #
    # @param name [String] the vessel
    # @return [true] when the vessel is in hand
    # @raise [SystemExit] when it is not
    def pick_up(name)
      fetch!(name, at_feet: true)
    end

    private

    # Calls DRCC.get_crafting_item. A plain get passes the four arguments
    # remedy has always passed; a guarded one adds skip_exit.
    #
    # @param name [String] the item
    # @param container [String, nil] the container named with it
    # @param items [Array<String>, nil] the items kept in +container+
    # @param belt [Hash, nil] the belt to look on
    # @param skip_exit [Boolean] return instead of exiting on a missing item
    # @return [Object] DRCC.get_crafting_item's return
    # @raise [SystemExit] when DRCC.get_crafting_item stops the script
    def crafting_get(name, container, items, belt, skip_exit)
      return DRCC.get_crafting_item(name, container, items, belt) unless skip_exit

      DRCC.get_crafting_item(name, container, items, belt, true)
    end

    # The last word of an item's name, which the game takes as the start of
    # the item's noun ({#held?}).
    #
    # @param name [String]
    # @return [String]
    def noun_of(name)
      name.split.last
    end

    # The hand that holds an item, found by the last word of its name with
    # DRCI.in_left_hand? and DRCI.in_right_hand? (lich-5 common-items.rb, in
    # every release from v5.20.0: :869/:879 at v5.20.0, :944/:954 at
    # v5.23.0), the checks DRCI.in_hands? makes for each hand. The left hand
    # is asked first.
    #
    # @param name [String] the name the item was asked for by
    # @return [Symbol, nil] :left, :right, or nil when neither hand holds it
    def hand_holding(name)
      noun = noun_of(name)
      if DRCI.in_left_hand?(noun)
        :left
      elsif DRCI.in_right_hand?(noun)
        :right
      end
    end

    # Whether both hands hold an item, found by the last word of its name
    # as {#hand_holding} finds it: two stacks of a herb.
    #
    # @param name [String] the name the item was asked for by
    # @return [Boolean]
    def in_both_hands?(name)
      noun = noun_of(name)
      DRCI.in_left_hand?(noun) && DRCI.in_right_hand?(noun)
    end

    # @param hand [Symbol] :left or :right
    # @return [String, nil] what the hand holds, as DRC names it (nil when
    #   empty)
    def contents(hand)
      hand == :left ? DRC.left_hand : DRC.right_hand
    end

    # Whether a name is routed as a herb: it equals herb1 or herb2.
    #
    # @param name [String, nil]
    # @return [Boolean]
    def herb?(name)
      @herbs.include?(name)
    end

    # Asks a question of the hands now and then once a second for +looks+
    # seconds, until it answers yes ({#got?}, {#released?}).
    #
    # @param looks [Integer] the looks after the first
    # @yieldreturn [Boolean] the answer the hands give now
    # @return [Boolean] true as soon as the block does; false after +looks+
    #   looks
    def within_late_looks(looks)
      return true if yield

      looks.times do
        pause 1
        return true if yield
      end
      false
    end
  end

  # Water or alcohol: where it comes from, and the command that adds it to
  # the vessel.
  #
  # With a vessel setting (water_container, alcohol_container) whose count
  # finds the liquid, or finds it once the vessel is rubbed and filled from
  # the crafting container, remedy pours from that vessel. Otherwise it puts
  # the plain liquid ("water", "alcohol") the crafting container holds.
  #
  # @!attribute [r] kind
  #   @return [String] 'water' or 'alcohol'
  # @!attribute [r] source
  #   @return [String] the vessel poured from, or the plain liquid (equal to
  #     {#kind}) put in
  # @!attribute [r] verb
  #   @return [String] 'pour' from a vessel, 'put' for the plain liquid
  #
  # Defined as a constant and reopened below without a superclass, as
  # {Order} is, so a second run in the same Lich session does not raise
  # "superclass mismatch for class Liquid".
  #
  # @api private
  Liquid = Data.define(:kind, :source, :verb)

  class Liquid
    # The verb for the plain liquid.
    PUT = 'put'

    # The verb for a vessel that holds the liquid.
    POUR = 'pour'

    # The end of the reply to the add that means the vessel refused the
    # liquid (the third of {Patterns::LIQUID_ADD_REPLIES}).
    #
    # @note code-derived (unverified): no log shows this reply.
    REFUSED = 'You cannot find a way to add that as an ingredient'

    # Finds where the liquid comes from, then puts the vessel away.
    #
    # With no vessel setting, sends nothing and answers the plain liquid.
    # With one, gets the vessel and counts the liquid in it; when the count
    # finds none, rubs the vessel, gets the liquid from the crafting
    # container, puts it in the vessel and counts again ({#refill?}). Any
    # reply but "I could not find what you were referring to." (25:13762),
    # a timeout included, means the vessel holds the liquid (as it
    # always has). Either way the vessel is put away by its name,
    # so an empty one does not stay in hand.
    #
    # @param kind [String] 'water' or 'alcohol'
    # @param vessel [String, nil] water_container or alcohol_container
    # @param inventory [Inventory] gets and stows the vessel
    # @return [Liquid] pouring from the vessel, or the plain liquid when
    #   both counts find nothing
    # @raise [SystemExit] when DRCC.get_crafting_item stops the script
    # @note code-derived (unverified): no log shows remedy count a liquid
    #   vessel. The rub's reply is real ("Brushing your fingers along a
    #   moonshine jug with five painted X's, a brief flash of light emanates
    #   from inside.", 26:22410, after the jug poured nothing at 26:22405);
    #   `get <liquid> from <bag>` and `put <liquid> in my <vessel>` (both
    #   without "my" before the bag or the liquid) are in no log.
    def self.find(kind, vessel, inventory)
      plain = new(kind: kind, source: kind, verb: PUT)
      return plain unless vessel

      poured = new(kind: kind, source: vessel, verb: POUR)
      inventory.get(vessel)
      found = poured.holds? || poured.refill?(inventory)
      inventory.stow(vessel)
      found ? poured : plain
    end

    # Whether the vessel's count finds the liquid.
    #
    # @return [Boolean] false only on "I could not find what you were
    #   referring to." (25:13762)
    def holds?
      DRC.bput("count #{kind} in my #{source}", *Patterns::VESSEL_COUNT_REPLIES) != Patterns::NO_SUCH_ITEM_REPLY
    end

    # Rubs the vessel (some make their liquid when rubbed: 26:22410), fills
    # it from the crafting container, then counts again ({#holds?}).
    #
    # @param inventory [Inventory] names the crafting container
    # @return [Boolean] whether the second count finds the liquid
    def refill?(inventory)
      fput("rub my #{source}")
      fput("get #{kind} from #{inventory.bag}") # in case the liquid is in the bag and the vessel cannot make it
      fput("put #{kind} in my #{source}")
      holds?
    end

    # Whether the source is the plain liquid, which remedy buys when none is
    # on hand.
    #
    # @return [Boolean]
    def plain?
      source == kind
    end

    # The Flag the game's request for this liquid sets ({FLAGS}).
    #
    # @return [String] 'remedy-water' or 'remedy-alcohol'
    def flag
      "remedy-#{kind}"
    end

    # Adds the liquid, already in hand, to the vessel: `put water in my
    # <vessel>` or `pour <vessel> in my <vessel>`.
    #
    # - "Pour what?" (26:22405): the vessel poured from is empty. Remedy
    #   refills it once ({#refill?}) and pours again; when the refill finds
    #   nothing, or the second pour gets "Pour what?" too, remedy stops
    #   instead of pouring nothing on every request for the liquid
    #   (D03).
    # - The crafting vessel refuses the liquid ({REFUSED}): remedy gets the
    #   liquid out of the vessel poured from (the plain liquid from the
    #   crafting container) and puts it in by name.
    #
    # @param container [String] the crafting vessel
    # @param inventory [Inventory] stows the hand, gets the plain liquid and
    #   names the crafting container
    # @return [void]
    # @raise [SystemExit] when the vessel poured from stays empty, or when
    #   DRCC.get_crafting_item stops the script
    def add(container, inventory)
      reply = add_to(container)
      reply = refill_and_add(container, inventory) if empty_vessel?(reply)
      recover(container, inventory) if reply.end_with?(REFUSED)
    end

    private

    # Sends the add.
    #
    # @param container [String] the crafting vessel
    # @return [String] bput's matched reply, '' on a timeout
    def add_to(container)
      DRC.bput("#{verb} #{source} in my #{container}", *Patterns::LIQUID_ADD_REPLIES)
    end

    # Whether the reply says the vessel poured from had nothing to pour.
    #
    # @param reply [String] the reply to {#add_to}
    # @return [Boolean] false for the plain liquid, which is not poured
    def empty_vessel?(reply)
      !plain? && reply.start_with?(Patterns::POUR_WHAT_REPLY)
    end

    # Refills the empty vessel poured from and pours once more.
    #
    # @param container [String] the crafting vessel
    # @param inventory [Inventory] names the crafting container
    # @return [String] the reply to the second pour
    # @raise [SystemExit] when the refill finds no liquid, or the second
    #   pour gets "Pour what?" (26:22405) again
    # @note code-derived (unverified): no log shows a refill while
    #   crafting; with both hands full the get from the crafting container
    #   may find no free hand, which leaves the rub to make the liquid.
    def refill_and_add(container, inventory)
      stop_empty(inventory) unless refill?(inventory)
      reply = add_to(container)
      stop_empty(inventory) if empty_vessel?(reply)
      reply
    end

    # Stops remedy: the vessel poured from has nothing to pour.
    #
    # @param inventory [Inventory] names the crafting container
    # @return [void] never returns
    # @raise [SystemExit] always ({Remedy.stop})
    def stop_empty(inventory)
      Remedy.stop("the #{source} has no #{kind} to pour, even after rubbing it and filling it from the #{inventory.bag}.")
    end

    # Puts the liquid in by name after the crafting vessel refused the add.
    # The plain liquid comes straight from the crafting container, with the
    # get every plain add starts with (`get my water from my botanist's
    # toolbag` -> "You get some water from inside your botanist's toolbag.",
    # 26:10824 -> 26:10825), not by asking for water from "water" (`get my
    # water from my water`, H18).
    #
    # @param container [String] the crafting vessel
    # @param inventory [Inventory] stows the hand and gets the plain liquid
    # @return [void]
    # @raise [SystemExit] when DRCC.get_crafting_item stops the script
    # @note code-derived (unverified): the refusal and the recovery after it
    #   appear in no log. When the crafting vessel refuses the plain liquid
    #   again, it is put back "in" the plain liquid (`put my water in my
    #   water`), as before.
    def recover(container, inventory)
      inventory.stow(DRC.left_hand)
      inventory.get(kind) if plain? || !DRCI.get_item?(kind, source)
      return if DRCI.put_away_item?(kind, container)

      waitrt?
      DRCI.put_away_item?(kind, source)
    end
  end

  # Buys the plain water, the plain alcohol and the coal nugget catalyst
  # remedy has none of, at the alchemy shop of the crafting town, then walks
  # back to where the crafting happens.
  #
  # The shop is the crafting town's (DRCC.crafting_hometown: force_crafting_town
  # when set, otherwise hometown), the town every other crafting script
  # works and banks in (forge.lic:412, smith.lic:393, craft.lic:16 with
  # craft.lic:213). Before layer 13 remedy always bought in hometown, which
  # sent a character whose force_crafting_town is another town to its
  # hometown's shop and bank (H09). When base-crafting.yaml has
  # no remedies shop for the crafting town, hometown's shop is used, as it
  # always was, so a run that buys today still buys; when neither town has
  # one, a purchase stops remedy with a Remedy line instead of the
  # NoMethodError it raised before. Nothing is bought, and nothing stops,
  # when every supply is on hand.
  #
  # After each purchase remedy walks to alchemy_room, or, when that is not
  # set, back to the room remedy was started in (before layer 13 it called
  # DRCT.walk_to(nil), which stays in the shop: H29).
  #
  # @api private
  class Supplies
    # The coins withdrawn before each purchase, as remedy always has.
    WITHDRAWAL = '5 silver'

    # The catalyst names remedy buys a coal nugget for.
    COAL = ['coal nugget', 'nugget'].freeze

    # What each supply is ordered as: the base-crafting.yaml remedies keys
    # (base-crafting.yaml:1388-1416, Crossing) of the room it is ordered in
    # and of its menu number, and the name it is stowed and looked for by.
    ORDERS = {
      'water'   => { room: 'stock-room', number: 'stock-number', item: 'water' }.freeze,
      'alcohol' => { room: 'stock-room', number: 'stock-number-a', item: 'alcohol' }.freeze,
      'coal'    => { room: 'catalyst-room', number: 'catalyst_number', item: 'coal nugget' }.freeze
    }.freeze

    # The town whose alchemy shop remedy buys in: the crafting town when
    # base-crafting.yaml has a remedies shop for it, otherwise hometown when
    # it has one.
    #
    # @param settings [OpenStruct] reads force_crafting_town and hometown
    # @param remedies [Hash{String => Hash}] base-crafting.yaml's remedies
    #   data, by town
    # @return [String, nil] the town, or nil when neither has a shop
    def self.shop_town(settings, remedies)
      [DRCC.crafting_hometown(settings), settings.hometown].find { |town| remedies[town] }
    end

    # Takes what the purchases need. Sends nothing to the game.
    #
    # @param settings [OpenStruct] the character's settings; reads
    #   force_crafting_town and hometown here, and the bank helper reads more
    # @param remedies [Hash{String => Hash}] base-crafting.yaml's remedies
    #   data, by town
    # @param inventory [Inventory] stows what is bought
    # @param return_room [Integer, String, nil] where to walk after a
    #   purchase: alchemy_room, or the room remedy was started in
    def initialize(settings:, remedies:, inventory:, return_room:)
      @settings = settings
      @crafting_town = DRCC.crafting_hometown(settings)
      @town = self.class.shop_town(settings, remedies)
      @stock = @town && remedies[@town]
      @inventory = inventory
      @return_room = return_room
    end

    # Buys plain water, plain alcohol and a coal nugget catalyst when none is
    # on hand.
    #
    # @param water [Liquid] the water source ({Liquid.find})
    # @param alcohol [Liquid] the alcohol source ({Liquid.find})
    # @param catalyst [String] the catalyst the recipe names
    # @return [void]
    # @raise [SystemExit] when a purchase is needed and no town has a shop
    #   ({#buy})
    # @note Water and alcohol are bought whenever they are plain and missing,
    #   whether or not the recipe uses them, as before (H20, a
    #   follow-up): remedy is not told the recipe's liquid.
    def buy_missing(water:, alcohol:, catalyst:)
      buy('water') if water.plain? && !DRCI.exists?(water.source)
      buy('alcohol') if alcohol.plain? && !DRCI.exists?(alcohol.source)
      buy('coal') if COAL.include?(catalyst) && !DRCI.exists?(catalyst)
    end

    private

    # Withdraws coins at the shop town's bank, orders the supply, puts it
    # away, checks it is on hand and walks back ({Supplies}).
    #
    # @param supply [String] a key of {ORDERS}
    # @return [void]
    # @raise [SystemExit] when no town has a shop ({#stop_no_shop})
    # @note A purchase that leaves nothing on hand (the bank had no coins
    #   for it, or the walk failed) is reported and remedy carries on, as it
    #   always has: the recipe may not use that supply (H20), and
    #   one that does stops at the guarded get of it.
    def buy(supply)
      order = ORDERS[supply]
      stop_no_shop(order[:item]) unless @stock

      DRCM.get_money_from_bank(WITHDRAWAL, @settings, @town)
      DRCT.order_item(@stock[order[:room]], @stock[order[:number]])
      @inventory.stow(order[:item])
      report_not_bought(order[:item]) unless DRCI.exists?(order[:item])
      DRCT.walk_to(@return_room)
    end

    # Prints that the purchase left nothing on hand.
    #
    # @param item [String] what was ordered
    # @return [void]
    def report_not_bought(item)
      DRC.message("Remedy: could not buy #{item} at the #{@town} alchemy shop; crafting on without it.")
    end

    # Stops remedy: a supply is missing and base-crafting.yaml has no
    # alchemy shop for the crafting town or hometown (Aesry, for one).
    #
    # @param item [String] the missing supply
    # @return [void] never returns
    # @raise [SystemExit] always ({Remedy.stop})
    def stop_no_shop(item)
      towns = [@crafting_town, @settings.hometown].uniq.join(' or ')
      Remedy.stop("no #{item} on hand, and no alchemy shop is known in #{towns} to buy it from: put #{item} in your crafting_container, or set force_crafting_town to a town with one.")
    end
  end

  # Gathers herb1 into the lowered vessel: gets stacks and combines them
  # until the counts reach the target, puts the stack in, puts away what is
  # left and picks the vessel up again. The target is the herb quantity the
  # book form was given ({Order#herb_quantity}), else {HANDFUL}.
  #
  # A gathering sends the commands remedy has always sent for it:
  # every count read after a get from herb_container or
  # the crafting container is added to the count so far, and after a
  # combine too large ("That stack of herbs is too large to add more to.",
  # 25:30006) both stacks are counted and one is put back only when the
  # other counts {FULL_STACK}. What changes is where that went wrong or
  # never ended:
  # - after a later get, a combine that finds one stack in hand ("You must
  #   be holding both substances to combine them.", 25:18266) means the get
  #   added nothing, so gathering ends at the count before it; outside the
  #   bags that reply used to be taken for 25 pieces (H04), in the
  #   bags the one stack's count used to be added again;
  # - outside the bags the count after a merge ("You combine the stacks of
  #   herbs together.", 25:11425) reads the one merged stack, so it replaces
  #   the count so far instead of being added to it;
  # - in the bags, after a merge, one more count reads the one merged stack
  #   and replaces the count so far (H05), unless the count before
  #   the merge already read the target by itself;
  # - in or outside the bags, with no herb quantity, a round whose combine
  #   does not merge goes by the sum of every count, as remedy has always
  #   done, so it ends where it ends today, even after a merge replaced the
  #   count so far (with a herb quantity, see below);
  # - at most as many gets as the target has pieces (H26): every
  #   get whose count is answered adds at least one piece, so that many
  #   gets reach the target whenever each does, and a get that adds nothing
  #   ends the gathering by itself (the combine finds one stack). The bound
  #   only stops gets whose counts and combines go unanswered; without it, a
  #   herb the bag lists but the get never brings was counted and combined
  #   forever. With no herb quantity that is {HANDFUL} gets, as before.
  #
  # The stack put back beside a full one still goes to the plain stow remedy
  # has always sent, with or without herb_container (CURRENT H27): that
  # gathering works today, so A5 keeps its commands.
  #
  # Short of a handful with no herb quantity, remedy says so and crafts with
  # what it holds, as it always has; the game decides ("requires more
  # pieces" stops remedy, {CraftLoop::MORE_PIECES}). Short of a herb
  # quantity, remedy puts the herb away, picks the vessel up again and
  # stops before the put, so nothing goes into the vessel: the herb
  # quantity is a minimum the caller asked for, and the whole stack in hand
  # still goes in when it is met (exact portions are H17's
  # follow-up).
  #
  # With a herb quantity the put takes one stack, so remedy goes only by a
  # count of the one stack it will put in, never by a sum of counts:
  # - the count of the first stack, or of the one stack a merge left (the
  #   one count after a merge in the bags is not replaced by the sum when
  #   it goes unanswered);
  # - after a combine too large, both stacks are counted by place and the
  #   one with fewer pieces is put back with the plain stow ({#keep_fuller});
  #   the gathering ends on the count of the stack kept, as no stack
  #   brought later can join a stack that the last one did not fit;
  # - after a later get whose combine goes unanswered, what is in hand is
  #   unknown, so the gathering ends uncounted and remedy stops short;
  # - a later get that finds the herb in neither bag leaves the stack from
  #   the bags in hand beside the one it brought, so the two are combined
  #   before anything is counted outside the bags ({#join_unrouted}).
  # A sum of counts can pass a herb quantity with no stack holding it: 46
  # held and 30 more too large to add (25:30006) sum to 76, and the put
  # would take 46 or 30 of them.
  #
  # A first get that leaves no herb in hand, or a
  # leftover that neither herb_container nor the crafting container takes,
  # stops remedy with the vessel picked up again. Those checks read the
  # hands (DRCI.in_hands?), which sends nothing, and look again for
  # {Inventory::STOP_LOOKS} seconds ({Inventory#got?},
  # {Inventory#released?}), so a get or stow answered up to 30 s after its
  # bput gave up (three 15 s silences, the bput's
  # own included) goes on as it always has.
  #
  # @note H05: with two stacks in hand the logs do not show which
  #   one `count` reads. If it reads the stack picked up first, adding it to
  #   the count so far counts a merged stack twice and remedy crafts with
  #   fewer pieces than the target. So after a merge in the bags
  #   ({#add_from_bag}) remedy counts the one merged stack once more and
  #   goes by that count, whichever stack the count before the merge read
  #   (a deliberate change: one extra `count my
  #   <herb>` in the combine path only).
  # @note code-derived (unverified): a herb found outside both bags is put
  #   into the vessel and taken back out for each later get, as remedy has
  #   always done ({#add_from_elsewhere}). A stop short of a herb quantity
  #   comes after the round that takes it back out, so the vessel is empty
  #   unless that get failed, which a run without a herb quantity would
  #   then craft on with. With a herb quantity the hands tell whether it
  #   failed (one stack in hand, not two), and the stop then says the herb
  #   may still be in the vessel instead of "nothing was put in".
  # @api private
  class HerbGatherer
    # The pieces of herb a remedy takes when no herb quantity is given, and
    # the target remedy has always gathered to.
    HANDFUL = 25

    # The largest stack of herbs the game makes: after "That stack of herbs
    # is too large to add more to." (25:30006) the count read a full stack,
    # "You count out 75 pieces of material there." (25:30012).
    FULL_STACK = 75

    # The text bput returns for "You combine the stacks of herbs together."
    # (25:11425): the two stacks are one.
    MERGED = 'You combine'

    # The text bput returns for "You must be holding both substances to
    # combine them." (25:18266): one stack in hand. After the first get that
    # is expected; after a later one, the get added nothing.
    HOLDING_BOTH = 'You must be holding both'

    # The end of the text bput returns for "That stack of herbs is too large
    # to add more to." (25:30006).
    TOO_LARGE = 'too large to add'

    # @param inventory [Inventory] routes herb1 as a herb
    # @param minimum [Integer, nil] the herb quantity ({Order#herb_quantity}):
    #   the fewest pieces to put in, stopping short of them; nil gathers
    #   {HANDFUL} and crafts with what is found
    def initialize(inventory, minimum: nil)
      @inventory = inventory
      @minimum = minimum
      @target = minimum || HANDFUL
      @left_in_vessel = false
    end

    # Gets the vessel, lowers it, gathers the herb, puts the herb in, puts
    # away what is left of it and picks the vessel up again. Short of a herb
    # quantity, puts the herb away and stops instead of the put
    # ({#stop_short}).
    #
    # @param herb [String] herb1
    # @param vessel [String] the alchemy_tools entry
    # @return [void]
    # @raise [SystemExit] when the vessel cannot be got or picked up again
    #   ({Inventory#fetch!}), when the first get leaves no herb in hand, when
    #   fewer pieces than the herb quantity are gathered, when the herb left
    #   after the put cannot be put away, or when DRCC.get_crafting_item
    #   stops the script
    def gather(herb, vessel)
      @inventory.fetch!(vessel)
      @inventory.lower(vessel)
      counted = collect(herb, vessel)
      stop_short(herb, vessel, counted) if @minimum && !enough?(counted)
      report_short(herb, counted)
      put_in(herb, vessel)
      @inventory.pick_up(vessel)
    end

    private

    # Gets stacks from herb_container or the crafting container, counting
    # and combining them, until gathering ends (see the class comment). A
    # stack found in neither goes on by {#gather_outside_bags}.
    #
    # Two tallies are kept, as outside the bags: the sum of every count,
    # which remedy has always gone by, and the count so far, which a merge
    # sets to the merged stack's count ({#add_from_bag}).
    #
    # @param herb [String] herb1
    # @param vessel [String] the alchemy_tools entry, lowered
    # With a herb quantity, a combine that leaves no count of one stack ends
    # the gathering ({#unsettled?}, {#held_after_unmerged}), and a later get
    # found in neither bag goes on by {#join_unrouted}, not straight to
    # {#gather_outside_bags}: the stack it brought is in the other hand.
    #
    # @return [Integer, nil] the pieces counted, nil when the game gave no
    #   count
    # @raise [SystemExit] when the first get leaves no herb in hand, or when
    #   DRCC.get_crafting_item stops the script
    def collect(herb, vessel)
      counted = summed = nil
      @target.times do |fetch|
        routed = @inventory.get(herb) != Inventory::UNROUTED
        empty_handed(herb, vessel) if fetch.zero? && !@inventory.got?(herb, looks: Inventory::STOP_LOOKS)
        return join_unrouted(herb, vessel, counted, fetch + 1) if !routed && @minimum && fetch.positive?
        return gather_outside_bags(herb, vessel, fetch + 1) unless routed

        count = count_held(herb)
        reply = combine(herb)
        return one_stack_count(count, counted) if fetch.positive? && reply == HOLDING_BOTH
        return held_after_unmerged(herb, reply) if unsettled?(reply, later: fetch.positive?)

        summed = sum(summed, count)
        counted = add_from_bag(herb, reply, count, summed)
        return counted if enough?(counted)
      end
      counted
    end

    # The count so far after a get from a bag, its count and its combine.
    #
    # After a merge ({MERGED}) it is the merged stack's count
    # ({#count_merged}). After any other reply (too large, one stack, or
    # none) it is the sum of every count, as remedy has always gone by, so
    # such a round ends where it ends today; after a combine too large, one
    # stack is put back first ({#put_back_beside_full}). Adding the count to
    # a count a merge set would count the merged stack twice whenever
    # `count` reads it again (H05's assumption).
    #
    # @param herb [String] herb1
    # @param reply [String] the combine's reply ({#combine})
    # @param count [Integer, nil] the count read before the combine
    # @param summed [Integer, nil] the sum of every count, this one included
    # @return [Integer, nil] the pieces counted (nil when no count was
    #   answered)
    def add_from_bag(herb, reply, count, summed)
      return count_merged(herb, count, summed) if reply.end_with?(MERGED)

      put_back_beside_full(herb) if reply.end_with?(TOO_LARGE)
      summed
    end

    # The pieces held after a merge in the bags ({MERGED}). With two stacks
    # in hand the count before the merge may have read either one
    # (H05), so the sum with the count so far can count the merged stack
    # twice. One more `count my <herb>` reads the one stack now in hand and
    # replaces the sum.
    #
    # That count is not sent when the count before the merge read the
    # target by itself: whichever stack it read, the merged one holds at
    # least as many, and the gathering ends on the sum as it always has.
    #
    # With no herb quantity, a count after the merge that holds no number
    # leaves the sum, as before. With one it leaves nil: the sum may count
    # the merged stack twice, so it cannot show the herb quantity is met,
    # and gathering goes on to the next get, whose merge is counted again.
    #
    # @param herb [String] herb1
    # @param count [Integer, nil] the count read before the merge
    # @param summed [Integer, nil] the sum of every count, that one included
    # @return [Integer, nil] the count of the merged stack; when that count
    #   is not sent, the sum; when it holds no number, the sum with no herb
    #   quantity and nil with one
    # @note code-derived (unverified): no log shows a count after a merge in
    #   the bags; the count's replies are those of every `count my <herb>`
    #   (25:12538).
    def count_merged(herb, count, summed)
      return summed if enough?(count)

      merged = count_held(herb)
      @minimum ? merged : merged || summed
    end

    # Gathers a herb DRCC found outside herb_container and the crafting
    # container: counts the stack in hand, then, until gathering ends, puts
    # it in the vessel, stows what is left, gets another stack, takes the
    # first one back out to combine them ({#add_from_elsewhere}) and counts.
    #
    # Two tallies are kept. The sum of every count is the one remedy has
    # always gone by. After a merge ({MERGED}) the count reads the one
    # merged stack, which holds the pieces counted before it, so that count
    # replaces the count so far. After any other reply (too large, or none)
    # the count goes on the sum of every count, as before, so a round that
    # does not merge ends where it ends today: adding it to a count a merge
    # replaced would count the stack taken back out of the vessel, already
    # in the merged count, a second time when `count` reads that stack.
    #
    # @param herb [String] herb1
    # @param vessel [String] the alchemy_tools entry, lowered
    # @param fetches [Integer] the gets already made
    # @return [Integer, nil] the pieces counted, nil when the game gave no
    #   count
    # @raise [SystemExit] when DRCC.get_crafting_item stops the script
    # With a herb quantity, a combine that does not merge ends the
    # gathering ({#unsettled?}, {#held_after_unmerged}) before that count.
    #
    # @note code-derived (unverified): with two stacks in hand the logs do
    #   not show which one `count` reads (H05), nor which one the
    #   put into the vessel takes.
    def gather_outside_bags(herb, vessel, fetches)
      counted = summed = count_held(herb)
      (@target - fetches).times do
        return counted if enough?(counted)

        reply = add_from_elsewhere(herb, vessel)
        return counted if reply == HOLDING_BOTH
        return held_after_unmerged(herb, reply) if unsettled?(reply, later: true)

        count = count_held(herb)
        summed = sum(summed, count)
        counted = reply.end_with?(MERGED) ? count : summed
      end
      counted
    end

    # With a herb quantity, goes on after a later get that found the herb in
    # neither bag (DRCI.exists? false for both, as when the bags hold no
    # more or a tap goes unanswered) and got it without naming a container.
    # The stack from the bags is still in hand, and the get may have put a
    # second stack in the other hand, so a `count my <herb>` could read
    # either one (H05), and {#gather_outside_bags}' first count
    # would be taken for the stack the put takes: the put could take fewer
    # pieces than the herb quantity, or a stack that holds it could be
    # stowed. So the stacks are combined first, and remedy goes, as after a
    # later get in the bags, only by a count of one stack:
    # - one stack in hand ({HOLDING_BOTH}): the get added nothing and the
    #   gathering ends at the count so far, the count of that stack;
    # - too large ({TOO_LARGE}): the stack with fewer pieces goes back by
    #   place and the gathering ends on the count of the one kept
    #   ({#keep_fuller});
    # - no reply: what is in hand is unknown, so the gathering ends
    #   uncounted ({#held_after_unmerged});
    # - merged ({MERGED}): one stack is in hand and {#gather_outside_bags}
    #   goes on from its count.
    #
    # @param herb [String] herb1
    # @param vessel [String] the alchemy_tools entry, lowered
    # @param counted [Integer, nil] the count so far, of the one stack held
    #   before the get
    # @param fetches [Integer] the gets already made, this one included
    # @return [Integer, nil] the pieces of the one stack in hand, nil when
    #   no count of one stack is left to go by
    # @raise [SystemExit] when DRCC.get_crafting_item stops the script
    # @note code-derived (unverified): no log shows a later get from
    #   outside the bags. Without a herb quantity the commands are unchanged
    #   ({#gather_outside_bags} at once).
    def join_unrouted(herb, vessel, counted, fetches)
      reply = combine(herb)
      return counted if reply == HOLDING_BOTH
      return held_after_unmerged(herb, reply) if unsettled?(reply, later: true)

      gather_outside_bags(herb, vessel, fetches)
    end

    # Gets another stack from wherever DRCI finds it and combines it with
    # the one held.
    #
    # The held stack goes into the vessel and the vessel is put away first,
    # so that the get does not take the herb from the vessel and the game
    # does not answer "You are already holding that."; the first stack is
    # then taken back out of the lowered vessel.
    #
    # @param herb [String] herb1
    # @param vessel [String] the alchemy_tools entry, lowered
    # @return [String] the combine's reply ({#combine}): {HOLDING_BOTH}
    #   means the get added nothing, and gathering is over
    # @raise [SystemExit] when DRCC.get_crafting_item stops the script
    # With a herb quantity, the hands then tell whether the first stack came
    # back out ({Inventory#got_second?}: both hands hold the herb), for the
    # stop's message ({#stop_short}). DRCI.get_item?'s answer cannot tell:
    # the hands already hold the herb got before it.
    #
    # @note code-derived (unverified): no log shows this sequence. `stow my
    #   <herb>` is kept as remedy has always sent it ("in case there are
    #   extra"), so a gathering that works today sends the same commands.
    def add_from_elsewhere(herb, vessel)
      DRCI.put_away_item?(herb, vessel)
      fput("stow my #{herb}")
      @inventory.get(vessel)
      @inventory.stow(vessel)
      DRCI.get_item?(herb)
      @inventory.get(vessel)
      @inventory.lower(vessel)
      DRCI.get_item?(herb, vessel)
      @left_in_vessel = @minimum && !@inventory.got_second?(herb)
      combine(herb)
    end

    # The pieces in hand when the combine after a later get in the bags
    # finds one stack ({HOLDING_BOTH}): the get added nothing. With no herb
    # quantity it is the count so far, as before. With one it is the count
    # read before that combine, which read the one stack in hand (so a
    # merged stack whose count went unanswered is counted now), else the
    # count so far.
    #
    # @param count [Integer, nil] the count read before the combine
    # @param counted [Integer, nil] the count so far
    # @return [Integer, nil]
    def one_stack_count(count, counted)
      @minimum && count ? count : counted
    end

    # Whether, with a herb quantity, a combine leaves no count of one stack
    # to go by, which ends the gathering: one too large ({TOO_LARGE}), or,
    # after a later get, any reply but a merge ({MERGED}). The one stack
    # reply ({HOLDING_BOTH}) after a later get ends it before this is asked.
    #
    # @param reply [String] the combine's reply ({#combine})
    # @param later [Boolean] whether the combine follows a later get
    # @return [Boolean] false with no herb quantity
    def unsettled?(reply, later:)
      return false unless @minimum

      reply.end_with?(TOO_LARGE) || (later && !reply.end_with?(MERGED))
    end

    # The pieces in hand, with a herb quantity, after a combine that ended
    # the gathering ({#unsettled?}): after one too large, the count of the
    # stack kept ({#keep_fuller}); after any other reply nil, as what is in
    # hand is unknown (one stack or two, merged or not), so remedy stops
    # short ({#stop_short}).
    #
    # @param herb [String] herb1
    # @param reply [String] the combine's reply ({#combine})
    # @return [Integer, nil]
    def held_after_unmerged(herb, reply)
      reply.end_with?(TOO_LARGE) ? keep_fuller(herb) : nil
    end

    # After a combine that would make too large a stack, counts both stacks
    # and puts back the one beside a full stack ({FULL_STACK}): the second
    # when the first is full, else the first when the second is. Puts
    # nothing back when neither count is full, as remedy always has.
    #
    # The stack is named by its place in hand ("first nemoih", the form of
    # `put my second clubm in rugu`, 25:29951 -> 25:29952) and put away
    # with the plain stow remedy has always sent: DRCI.put_away_item? with
    # no container sends `stow my <name>`, with or without herb_container
    # (CURRENT H27, kept on purpose).
    #
    # @param herb [String] herb1
    # @return [void]
    def put_back_beside_full(herb)
      first, second = count_by_place(herb)
      if first == FULL_STACK
        DRCI.put_away_item?("second #{herb}")
      elsif second == FULL_STACK
        DRCI.put_away_item?("first #{herb}")
      end
    end

    # With a herb quantity, after a combine too large: counts both stacks by
    # place, puts back the one with fewer pieces (the second when they are
    # equal) with the plain stow {#put_back_beside_full} sends, and goes by
    # the count of the one kept. Beside a full stack ({FULL_STACK}) that is
    # the stack {#put_back_beside_full} puts back, by the same command.
    #
    # The hands tell whether the put-back worked ({Inventory#released_one?}):
    # lich-5 counts "That stack of herbs is too large to add more to."
    # (26:32077) as stowed (F3).
    #
    # @param herb [String] herb1
    # @return [Integer, nil] the pieces of the stack kept in hand; nil when
    #   either count holds no number (nothing is put back) or both stacks
    #   are still in hand after the put-back
    def keep_fuller(herb)
      first, second = count_by_place(herb)
      return nil unless first && second

      place, kept = first >= second ? ['second', first] : ['first', second]
      DRCI.put_away_item?("#{place} #{herb}")
      kept if @inventory.released_one?(herb)
    end

    # Counts the two stacks in hand by their places ("You count out 75
    # pieces of material there.", 25:30012, after `count my first nemoih`).
    #
    # @param herb [String] herb1
    # @return [Array(Integer, Integer)] the first stack's and the second's
    #   pieces, each nil when its reply holds no number
    def count_by_place(herb)
      [Remedy.counted("count my first #{herb}", Patterns::FIRST_STACK_COUNT_REPLIES),
       Remedy.counted("count my second #{herb}", Patterns::SECOND_STACK_COUNT_REPLIES)]
    end

    # @param counted [Integer, nil] the pieces counted so far
    # @param count [Integer, nil] a new count
    # @return [Integer, nil] their sum, a missing count adding nothing; nil
    #   when neither holds a number
    def sum(counted, count)
      return counted if count.nil?

      (counted || 0) + count
    end

    # @param herb [String] herb1
    # @return [Integer, nil] the count of the herb in hand (25:12538), nil
    #   when the reply holds no number
    def count_held(herb)
      Remedy.counted("count my #{herb}", Patterns::HERB_COUNT_REPLIES)
    end

    # @param herb [String] herb1
    # @return [String] the combine's reply (bput's text; '' on no reply)
    def combine(herb)
      DRC.bput("combine my #{herb}", *Patterns::COMBINE_REPLIES)
    end

    # @param counted [Integer, nil] the pieces counted
    # @return [Boolean] whether they reach the target (the herb quantity,
    #   else {HANDFUL})
    def enough?(counted)
      !counted.nil? && counted >= @target
    end

    # Stops remedy when the first get left no herb in hand: picks the vessel
    # up again, so the stop leaves it in hand like every other stop.
    #
    # @param herb [String] herb1
    # @param vessel [String] the alchemy_tools entry, lowered
    # @return [void] never returns
    # @raise [SystemExit] always
    def empty_handed(herb, vessel)
      @inventory.pick_up(vessel)
      Remedy.stop("could not get #{herb}.")
    end

    # Stops remedy when gathering ended short of the herb quantity, before
    # anything is put in the vessel: puts the herb in hand away
    # ({Inventory#stow_leftover}: herb_container, then the crafting
    # container) and picks the vessel up again, so the stop leaves it in
    # hand, empty, like every other stop before crafting. When a stack put
    # in the vessel to get one outside the bags did not come back out
    # ({#add_from_elsewhere}), the message says it may still be there.
    #
    # @param herb [String] herb1
    # @param vessel [String] the alchemy_tools entry, lowered
    # @param counted [Integer, nil] the pieces counted, nil when the game
    #   gave no count
    # @return [void] never returns
    # @raise [SystemExit] always
    def stop_short(herb, vessel, counted)
      kept = @inventory.stow_leftover(herb) ? '' : " The #{herb} could not be put away and is still in hand."
      @inventory.pick_up(vessel)
      found = counted ? "have #{counted}" : "could not count the #{herb} gathered"
      vessel_state = @left_in_vessel ? "the #{herb} put in the #{vessel} to get another stack may still be in it" : "nothing was put in the #{vessel}"
      Remedy.stop("need #{@minimum} pieces of #{herb} (the herb quantity), #{found}; #{vessel_state}.#{kept}")
    end

    # Says so when gathering ended short of a handful with no herb quantity;
    # remedy crafts on with what it holds, as it always has.
    #
    # @param herb [String] herb1
    # @param counted [Integer, nil] the pieces counted
    # @return [void]
    def report_short(herb, counted)
      return if enough?(counted)

      found = counted ? "gathered only #{counted} pieces of #{herb}" : "could not count the #{herb} gathered"
      DRC.message("Remedy: #{found} (a remedy takes #{HANDFUL}); crafting with what is in hand.")
    end

    # Puts the herb in the vessel; tilts the vessel out first when it
    # already holds something. Puts away what is left of the herb.
    #
    # @param herb [String] herb1
    # @param vessel [String] the alchemy_tools entry
    # @return [void]
    # @raise [SystemExit] when the herb left cannot be put away
    #   ({#put_away_leftover})
    def put_in(herb, vessel)
      case put_herb(herb, vessel, Patterns::HERB_PUT_REPLIES)
      when 'You put', /can only hold\z/, /You vigorously rub\z/
        put_away_leftover(herb, vessel)
      when /already has something\z/, 'You realize the'
        DRC.bput("tilt my #{vessel}", *Patterns::TILT_REPLIES)
        DRC.bput("tilt my #{vessel}", *Patterns::TILT_AGAIN_REPLIES)
        put_herb(herb, vessel, Patterns::HERB_PUT_AGAIN_REPLIES)
        put_away_leftover(herb, vessel)
      end
    end

    # Puts the herb in the vessel and returns bput's reply.
    #
    # When the herb stack in hand has an id ({Inventory#held_id}, the hand
    # chosen by matching the herb), the put is `put #<id> in my <vessel>`,
    # listening for +replies+ and for {Patterns::NOT_FOUND}. Should the game
    # answer "What were you referring to?" (a stale id, for one), the put is
    # sent once more as remedy's old command, `put <herb> in my <vessel>`,
    # with +replies+ alone, and that reply is returned: so a refused id
    # leaves remedy exactly where the old command would have. With no id,
    # or no hand holding the herb, only the old command is sent, byte for
    # byte and with the same replies as before.
    #
    # The old command names the herb with no "my" (H32), so the
    # game may take a same-noun item other than the stack in hand. The id
    # names the very stack remedy gathered, whatever else shares its
    # noun. The id is
    # read for each put, so the put after a tilt names the stack then in
    # hand.
    #
    # Only a refusal sends the old command after the id form. A put that
    # bput gave up on (no reply) is not resent: the game may yet have taken
    # the stack, and a second put by name could then take a same-noun item
    # (H32). That case ends as it did with the old command alone.
    #
    # @param herb [String] herb1, as remedy gathered it
    # @param vessel [String] the alchemy_tools entry
    # @param replies [Array<Regexp>] {Patterns::HERB_PUT_REPLIES}, or
    #   {Patterns::HERB_PUT_AGAIN_REPLIES} after a tilt
    # @return [String] the text bput returned for the last put sent
    # @note The id is GameObj's at the moment of the put. A hand tag the
    #   game has not yet sent for the last combine would leave a stale id;
    #   no log carries hand tags (Genie client logs), so this is code-derived, as
    #   is the refusal of a stale id: no log sends the id form.
    #   "What were you referring to?" is the game's answer to a put of an
    #   item it cannot find (26:39192 -> 26:39194).
    def put_herb(herb, vessel, replies)
      id = @inventory.held_id(herb)
      if id
        reply = DRC.bput("put ##{id} in my #{vessel}", *replies, *Patterns::NOT_FOUND)
        return reply unless reply.start_with?(Patterns::NOT_FOUND_REPLY)
      end
      DRC.bput("put #{herb} in my #{vessel}", *replies)
    end

    # Puts away the herb left in hand after the put ({Inventory#stow_leftover}),
    # for a stack of more than the vessel took. When the hands still hold it,
    # crafting would find no free hand for the stirrer (D12), so
    # remedy picks the vessel up and stops.
    #
    # @param herb [String] herb1
    # @param vessel [String] the alchemy_tools entry, lowered
    # @return [void]
    # @raise [SystemExit] when the herb is still in hand
    def put_away_leftover(herb, vessel)
      return if @inventory.stow_leftover(herb)

      @inventory.pick_up(vessel)
      Remedy.stop("could not put away the #{herb} left in hand after the put, and crafting needs that hand.")
    end
  end

  # What remedy does when the game asks for an ingredient or a step: one
  # private method per {FLAGS} entry, run through {TABLE}.
  #
  # Each method resets its own Flag just before it sends its command, so a
  # cue that comes in the command's own reply is kept for the next pass.
  # One that brings an ingredient or the sieve puts away the stirrer, gets
  # the item, and gets the stirrer back afterwards. Every get but a
  # liquid's is guarded ({Inventory#fetch!}); the catalyst and herb2 puts
  # stop remedy when the game finds no such item.
  #
  # @api private
  class Handlers
    # Flag => the method that answers it, in {FLAGS} order: ingredients
    # before steps.
    TABLE = {
      'remedy-water'    => :add_water,
      'remedy-alcohol'  => :add_alcohol,
      'remedy-catalyst' => :add_catalyst,
      'remedy-herb'     => :add_herb,
      'remedy-sieve'    => :sieve,
      'remedy-turn'     => :turn,
      'remedy-smell'    => :smell
    }.freeze

    # The tool a sieve step is done with.
    SIEVE = 'sieve'

    # @param order [Order] names the catalyst, herb2 and the product
    # @param tools [Tools] the vessel and the stirrer
    # @param inventory [Inventory] the gets and stows
    # @param water [Liquid] where water comes from
    # @param alcohol [Liquid] where alcohol comes from
    def initialize(order:, tools:, inventory:, water:, alcohol:)
      @order = order
      @tools = tools
      @inventory = inventory
      @water = water
      @alcohol = alcohol
    end

    # The first Flag in {TABLE} order that is set.
    #
    # @return [String, nil] the Flag's name; nil when none is set
    def pending
      TABLE.each_key.find { |flag| Flags[flag] }
    end

    # Answers a Flag.
    #
    # @param flag [String] a {TABLE} key
    # @return [void]
    # @raise [KeyError] for a name not in {TABLE}
    # @raise [SystemExit] when an item cannot be got or put in, when the game
    #   asks for a second herb the order does not name, or when
    #   DRCC.get_crafting_item stops the script
    def run(flag)
      send(TABLE.fetch(flag))
    end

    private

    # Adds water ({Patterns::NEED_WATER}).
    #
    # @return [void]
    def add_water
      add_liquid(@water)
    end

    # Adds alcohol ({Patterns::NEED_ALCOHOL}).
    #
    # @return [void]
    def add_alcohol
      add_liquid(@alcohol)
    end

    # Adds the catalyst ({Patterns::NEED_CATALYST}): "You vigorously rub the
    # ingot alongside the mortar ..." (25:11081).
    #
    # @return [void]
    # @raise [SystemExit] when the catalyst cannot be got or put in
    def add_catalyst
      with_item(@order.catalyst, 'remedy-catalyst') { put_ingredient(@order.catalyst) }
    end

    # Adds the second herb ({Patterns::NEED_HERB}). Stops, getting nothing,
    # when herb2 names none ({Order#second_herb}): there is no herb to add,
    # and asking for one by the name "na" can only fail. The stop says why
    # in the order's own terms ({Order#no_second_herb}).
    #
    # @return [void]
    # @raise [SystemExit] when herb2 names no herb, or when it cannot be got
    #   or put in
    # @note code-derived (unverified): no log shows the cue or this put.
    def add_herb
      herb = @order.second_herb
      Remedy.stop("the game asks for a second herb, and #{@order.no_second_herb}.") unless herb

      with_item(herb, 'remedy-herb') { put_ingredient(herb) }
    end

    # Pushes the product with the sieve ({Patterns::NEED_SIEVE}).
    #
    # @return [void]
    def sieve
      with_item(SIEVE, 'remedy-sieve') do
        DRC.bput("push my #{@order.noun} with my sieve", *Patterns::STEP_REPLIES)
      end
    end

    # Turns the vessel ({Patterns::NEED_TURN}).
    #
    # @return [void]
    def turn
      step('remedy-turn') { DRC.bput("turn my #{@tools.container}", *Patterns::STEP_REPLIES) }
    end

    # Smells the product ({Patterns::NEED_SMELL}).
    #
    # @return [void]
    def smell
      step('remedy-smell') { DRC.bput("smell my #{@order.noun}", *Patterns::STEP_REPLIES) }
    end

    # Gets the liquid's source and adds it ({Liquid#add}). The source's get
    # is not guarded: the plain liquid's name ("water",
    # "alcohol") is not proven to be in the name a hand shows, and the
    # add's reply already says whether it worked.
    #
    # @param liquid [Liquid]
    # @return [void]
    def add_liquid(liquid)
      with_item(liquid.source, liquid.flag, guarded: false) { liquid.add(@tools.container, @inventory) }
    end

    # Puts the catalyst or the second herb, in hand, into the vessel.
    #
    # @param item [String] the catalyst or herb2
    # @return [void]
    # @raise [SystemExit] when the game answers {Patterns::NOT_FOUND}
    #   (26:39194)
    def put_ingredient(item)
      reply = DRC.bput("put my #{item} in my #{@tools.container}", *Patterns::INGREDIENT_PUT_REPLIES)
      return unless reply.start_with?(Patterns::NOT_FOUND_REPLY)

      Remedy.stop("the game found no #{item} to put in the #{@tools.container}.")
    end

    # Puts away the stirrer, gets the item, resets the Flag, runs the block,
    # then puts the item away and gets the stirrer back. Both stows put away
    # the hand that does not hold the vessel ({Inventory#stow_beside}), so
    # the vessel stays in hand whichever hand holds it.
    #
    # @param item [String] what the block uses
    # @param flag [String] the Flag being answered
    # @param guarded [Boolean] stop when the item is not in hand after its
    #   get ({Inventory#fetch!}); false for a liquid's source
    # @yield sends the command
    # @return [void]
    # @raise [SystemExit] when the item (guarded) or the stirrer cannot be
    #   got, or when DRCC.get_crafting_item stops the script
    def with_item(item, flag, guarded: true)
      waitrt?
      @inventory.stow_beside(@tools.container)
      guarded ? @inventory.fetch!(item) : @inventory.get(item)
      Flags.reset(flag)
      yield
      waitrt?
      @inventory.stow_beside(@tools.container)
      @inventory.fetch!(@tools.stirrer)
    end

    # Resets the Flag and runs the block, which needs no other item.
    #
    # @param flag [String] the Flag being answered
    # @yield sends the command
    # @return [void]
    def step(flag)
      waitrt?
      Flags.reset(flag)
      yield
      waitrt?
    end
  end

  # The crafting loop: each pass answers the first Flag that is set
  # ({Handlers}), or else sends the crafting command and reads the reply,
  # until the game says the remedy is done.
  #
  # The game says so with the final-touches line, which comes after the
  # Roundtime line of the command that finished the remedy (26:6740 then
  # 26:6742). A bput that returns on Roundtime never reads it, so the loop
  # used to send the crafting command once more and finish on the game's
  # "Interesting thought really... but no." (26:11368) or "Try as you might,
  # you just can't mix" (26:6773) instead (D05, H16).
  # {DONE_FLAG} catches the line as it arrives, whatever command drew it,
  # and every pass checks it first; {ALREADY_FINISHED} stays as the
  # fallback.
  #
  # It replaces the method that called itself once a pass, whose stack ran
  # out after about 10,846 passes (H01). Two bounds end a run the
  # game no longer moves forward: {MAX_SILENT_REPLIES} (the game stopped
  # answering the command: H01, D06) and {MAX_CRAFT_STEPS} (the remedy
  # never gets done). A refused herb ({UNSUITABLE_HERB}, {MORE_PIECES}: H02,
  # D08) and instructions that faded before crafting began ({FADED_FLAG})
  # end it at once.
  #
  # @api private
  class CraftLoop
    # Crafting commands sent, all told, before the loop gives up on a remedy
    # that is never done.
    #
    # The 77 finished runs of the logs sent at most 17 crafting commands
    # (crush or mix) and 23 sends of every kind (a tally of both logs). No
    # log shows a recipe with a second herb, whose ingredient step is a
    # handler and not counted here; 100 leaves room for it, for resends
    # after silence and for the slowest recipe, while still ending a run
    # that would never finish.
    #
    # It is the only bound on a loop in which every command is answered
    # but the remedy never moves forward, since an answer counts the
    # silence afresh ({MAX_SILENT_REPLIES}): a Roundtime alone, or a need
    # cue whose handler sends its step without learning that it failed.
    # An empty liquid vessel is such a loop while the pour's reply goes
    # unread (D02/D03): each crush draws the alcohol cue alone
    # (as 25:10873), each pour gets "Pour what?" (26:22405), and the loop
    # ends here after 100 crushes and 99 pours, about 50 minutes when each
    # cue-only reply is a 15 s stall (D04). The stop then names
    # the step count, not the missing liquid.
    MAX_CRAFT_STEPS = 100

    # Crafting commands in a row that may go unanswered before the loop
    # gives up; the next one unanswered ends it.
    #
    # An unanswered command is a bput that matched nothing for 15 s and
    # brought no cue either. A Roundtime, a cue or a handled step counts the
    # silence afresh. In the logs the game's first line answered 2,231 of
    # the 2,235 crush and mix commands within 1 s, and the slowest within
    # 3 s ("Crush what?", 25:109873-109875); no crafting command ever timed
    # out (the logs' one bput timeout, 26:68670, is a cast). So 3 silences
    # in a row, 45 s with no reply, is a game that stopped answering, not
    # lag; the fourth ends the run after 60 s.
    MAX_SILENT_REPLIES = 3

    # The text bput returns for "Applying the final touches, you complete
    # working on a belradi elixir." (26:6742) when it is the first line of a
    # reply that matches: the line came before any Roundtime line. The same
    # line after a Roundtime sets {DONE_FLAG} ({Patterns::FINAL_TOUCHES}).
    FINAL_TOUCHES = 'Applying the final touches'

    # The ends of the texts bput returns for "Interesting thought really...
    # but no." (25:11264) and "Try as you might, you just can't mix the
    # tonic." (25:58002): the remedy is already done.
    ALREADY_FINISHED = [/Interesting thought really... but no.\z/, /you just can't mix\z/, /Try as you might\z/].freeze

    # The end of the text bput returns for "That craftable requires more
    # pieces of that plant to begin": the herb in the vessel is too little
    # to begin the remedy.
    #
    # @note code-derived (unverified): in no log; kept from remedy.lic:290 at
    #   03f11b54, which took it as done.
    MORE_PIECES = /That craftable requires more pieces of that plant to begin\z/

    # The end of the text bput returns for "You need a more suitable type of
    # herb if you want to craft that." (25:50933): the game refuses the herb
    # in the vessel. A resend leaves the same herb in the vessel.
    #
    # @note What a resend draws is in no log: after 25:50933 the logged
    #   character sent no crafting command (the next crafting line is the
    #   fade, 25:51002).
    UNSUITABLE_HERB = /You need a more suitable type of herb\z/

    # The end of the text bput returns for "Roundtime: 7 sec." (25:10825).
    ROUNDTIME = /Roundtime:\z/

    # The outcomes of {#run} that leave a begun remedy unfinished in the
    # vessel ({#resumable?}): the game stopped answering, or the step bound
    # was reached. :done, :unsuitable_herb, :more_pieces and :faded leave
    # nothing to carry on.
    OUTCOMES_LEFT_UNFINISHED = %i[unanswered too_long].freeze

    # The crafting command the loop sends next. After {#run} returns
    # :unanswered it is the command the game did not answer.
    #
    # @return [String, nil] nil before {#run}
    attr_reader :command

    # @param tools [Tools] makes the next crafting command
    # @param noun [String] the product noun
    # @param handlers [Handlers] answers the Flags
    # @param magic [Magic] runs the crafting magic routine
    def initialize(tools:, noun:, handlers:, magic:)
      @tools = tools
      @noun = noun
      @handlers = handlers
      @magic = magic
      @command = nil
      @begun = false
      @outcome = nil
    end

    # Whether the vessel holds a remedy the game has begun and not finished,
    # which `;remedy continue` can carry on: crafting has begun ({#run}) and
    # the loop has not ended on the remedy done or the herb refused. A loop
    # stopped from inside, by a handler or a commons helper, has no outcome
    # and counts as unfinished.
    #
    # @return [Boolean] false before {#run}
    def resumable?
      @begun && (@outcome.nil? || OUTCOMES_LEFT_UNFINISHED.include?(@outcome))
    end

    # Crafts until the game says the remedy is done, refuses it, or a bound
    # is reached.
    #
    # Resets {DONE_FLAG} first, so only a final-touches line that arrives
    # during this run can end it. Every pass waits out roundtime and runs
    # the magic routine once, as before; then, when {DONE_FLAG} is set, the
    # remedy is done and nothing more is sent; otherwise the pass does its
    # work. Crafting has begun once the game answers a crafting
    # command with Roundtime or asks for an ingredient or a step (each cue
    # names "an unfinished" remedy, e.g. 26:39150); in the continue form it
    # has begun before the loop starts. Until then, after a study, each pass
    # checks {FADED_FLAG} before it sends.
    #
    # @param command [String] the first crafting command
    # @param studied [Boolean] the recipe was studied for this run (the book
    #   form), and {FADED_FLAG} was reset after the study; false in the
    #   continue form, which studies nothing and resumes a remedy already
    #   begun
    # @return [Symbol] :done when the remedy is done (the final-touches line
    #   arrived, or the reply was {FINAL_TOUCHES} or {ALREADY_FINISHED});
    #   :unsuitable_herb or
    #   :more_pieces when the game refused the herb; :faded when the studied
    #   instructions faded before crafting began; :unanswered when more than
    #   {MAX_SILENT_REPLIES} commands in a row went unanswered; :too_long
    #   after {MAX_CRAFT_STEPS} crafting commands
    # @raise [SystemExit] when a handler stops the script ({Handlers#run})
    # @note code-derived (unverified): that a crafting command fails once
    #   the instructions have faded ({Patterns::INSTRUCTIONS_FADED}). The
    #   stop comes before the command is sent, so remedy never sees the
    #   game's answer; and should an earlier study's fade arrive after this
    #   run's study, remedy cannot tell it from this study's. Both are open
    #   game checks: if the game still takes the command, the stop would
    #   end a run that crafts today.
    # @note {DONE_FLAG} is read once the command's roundtime is waited out;
    #   in every logged case the final-touches line came in the same second
    #   as its Roundtime line (26:6740-6742, 26:11331-11333), well inside a
    #   roundtime of 7 s or more. Were it to come after the roundtime
    #   ended, the next pass would send the crafting command once more and
    #   end on {ALREADY_FINISHED}, as remedy did before.
    def run(command, studied: false)
      @command = command
      @begun = !studied
      Flags.reset(DONE_FLAG)
      @outcome = craft
    end

    private

    # The passes of {#run}.
    #
    # @return [Symbol] as {#run}
    # @raise [SystemExit] when a handler stops the script ({Handlers#run})
    def craft
      sends = 0
      silent = 0
      loop do
        waitrt?
        @magic.routine
        return :done if done?
        if (flag = @handlers.pending)
          @handlers.run(flag)
          silent = 0
          @begun = true
          next
        end
        return :faded if !@begun && Flags[FADED_FLAG]

        case (reply = classify(DRC.bput(@command, *Patterns::CRAFT_REPLIES)))
        when :done, :unsuitable_herb, :more_pieces
          return reply
        when :progress
          waitrt?
          @command = @tools.next_command(@noun)
          silent = 0
          @begun = true
        when :silent
          # A cue in the reply is an answer: the next pass handles it.
          silent += 1 unless @handlers.pending
        end
        sends += 1
        # The final touches came after this command's Roundtime: the next
        # pass ends as :done, ahead of the bounds.
        next if done?
        return :unanswered if silent > MAX_SILENT_REPLIES
        return :too_long if sends >= MAX_CRAFT_STEPS
      end
    end

    # Whether the final-touches line has arrived since {#run} began
    # ({DONE_FLAG}).
    #
    # @return [Boolean]
    def done?
      Flags[DONE_FLAG] ? true : false
    end

    # What a crafting command's reply means.
    #
    # @param reply [String] the text bput returned for {Patterns::CRAFT_REPLIES}
    # @return [Symbol] :done (finished now or before), :unsuitable_herb or
    #   :more_pieces (the herb refused), :progress (Roundtime) or :silent (no
    #   reply: '' after bput's 15 s)
    def classify(reply)
      case reply
      when FINAL_TOUCHES, *ALREADY_FINISHED then :done
      when UNSUITABLE_HERB then :unsuitable_herb
      when MORE_PIECES then :more_pieces
      when ROUNDTIME then :progress
      else :silent
      end
    end
  end

  # Takes the finished remedy out of the vessel, checks it is in hand, puts
  # the tools away, marks the product and reports how many uses it holds.
  # The product stays in hand.
  #
  # @api private
  class Finisher
    # The tool that marks the product.
    STAMP = 'stamp'

    # @param inventory [Inventory] the stows, the stamp's get and the hand
    #   that holds the product
    # @param tools [Tools] the vessel
    # @param noun [String] the product noun
    # @param mark [Boolean, nil] mark_crafted_goods
    # @param repair [StampRepair, nil] repairs a stamp too badly damaged to
    #   mark, when remedy_repair_stamp is set; nil (the default) leaves the
    #   product unmarked and says why
    def initialize(inventory:, tools:, noun:, mark:, repair: nil)
      @inventory = inventory
      @tools = tools
      @noun = noun
      @mark = mark
      @repair = repair
    end

    # Puts away the stirrer ({Inventory#stow_beside}), takes the product out
    # of the vessel ({Patterns::PRODUCT_TAKEN}, 26:11389), puts the vessel
    # away by its name, or the right hand when neither hand shows the vessel
    # ({Inventory#stow_vessel}), marks the product when mark_crafted_goods
    # is set, then counts it and reports the yield ({#report_yield}).
    #
    # The pattern-less fput returns the first line after the get, which is
    # not always the game's reply: " * Venras joins the adventure."
    # (25:27826) and "Osrel Meraud  (0%)" (26:27350) each came between a
    # product get and its "You get ..." reply. So when that line is not the
    # reply, the hands are looked at for {Inventory::LATE_GET_LOOKS} seconds
    # ({Inventory#got?}), as for every guarded get.
    #
    # When the product is not in hand after the get, remedy stops with the
    # vessel still in hand and the stirrer put away: the game has said the
    # remedy is done ({CraftLoop::ALREADY_FINISHED} also answers a vessel
    # with no remedy in it, H03), and the callers take the product
    # in hand as the sign it was made (D09). The vessel and the
    # stirrer go by name, so in the continue form with the vessel in the
    # left hand the vessel is not put away before the product is out, and
    # the product is never put away (H14).
    #
    # @return [void]
    # @raise [SystemExit] when the product is not in hand after the get
    #   ({Remedy.stop}), or as {Inventory#fetch?} may, on the stamp's get,
    #   or as {#stamp} may, when the stamp is repaired
    # @note The count is one command more than remedy sent before layer 17
    #   (a deliberate, always-on change). Under workorders it repeats
    #   the count workorders makes of the bundled remedy (workorders.lic,
    #   `count my first <noun>`); it changes nothing in hand.
    # @note The vessel is found by the last word of its alchemy_tools name.
    #   In the continue form it may be in neither hand (still tied on the
    #   belt: the product get works from there, 26:11389, 26:6794, and the
    #   crafting commands of the mortar form never name it); the right hand,
    #   which then holds the stirrer, is put away instead, as before
    #   ({Inventory#stow_vessel}).
    def finish
      waitrt?
      @inventory.stow_beside(@tools.container)
      reply = fput("get my #{@noun} from my #{@tools.container}")
      Remedy.stop("the #{@noun} is not in hand after taking it out of the #{@tools.container}.") unless taken?(reply)
      @inventory.stow_vessel(@tools.container)
      stamp if @mark
      report_yield
    end

    private

    # Whether the product is in hand after its get: the first line fput
    # returned says it was handed over ({Patterns::PRODUCT_TAKEN}), or a hand
    # holds the product noun now or within {Inventory::LATE_GET_LOOKS}
    # seconds ({Inventory#got?}).
    #
    # The hands are looked at again because that first line may be an
    # unrelated one that arrived before the reply (25:27826 between the get
    # at 25:27825 and its reply at 25:27830; 26:27350 between 26:27349 and
    # 26:27353). The hands then change only when the reply's own line is
    # parsed (lich-5 games.rb:1139-1153), after fput has returned
    # (global_defs.rb:1625-1628).
    #
    # @param reply [String, false] the first line fput returned for the get
    #   (false on a refusal or a timeout)
    # @return [Boolean] true at once on the reply line or a hand holding the
    #   product; false only after {Inventory::LATE_GET_LOOKS} looks find it
    #   in neither hand
    def taken?(reply)
      Patterns::PRODUCT_TAKEN.any? { |pattern| pattern.match?(reply.to_s) } || @inventory.got?(@noun)
    end

    # Marks the product with the stamp ({#mark}). When the game says the
    # stamp is too badly damaged ({Patterns::STAMP_DAMAGED}):
    # - with remedy_repair_stamp off (no {StampRepair}, the default), says
    #   so and leaves the product unmarked, sending what remedy always sent
    #   (D10: remedy used to say nothing);
    # - with it on, has {StampRepair#repair} repair the stamp, then marks
    #   once more; a stamp still damaged after that is reported, and the
    #   product left unmarked, with no further repair.
    # The product is made and in hand either way, so remedy still finishes.
    #
    # @return [void]
    # @raise [SystemExit] when the product cannot be got back after the
    #   repair ({StampRepair#repair}), or when a commons helper of the
    #   repair stops the script
    # @note code-derived (unverified): no log has "too badly damaged".
    def stamp
      return unless mark == :damaged
      return report_damaged unless @repair
      return unless @repair.repair(@noun)

      DRC.message("Remedy: the stamp is still too badly damaged after its repair; the #{@noun} is left unmarked.") if mark == :damaged
    end

    # Gets the stamp, marks the product with it ("You hold the liniment
    # steady and score the surface with the stamp.", 25:11294) and puts the
    # stamp away. When the stamp cannot be got ({Inventory#fetch?}), says so
    # and sends no mark.
    #
    # @return [Symbol] :damaged when the game says the stamp is too badly
    #   damaged ({Patterns::STAMP_DAMAGED}); :no_stamp when it could not be
    #   got; :sent for any other reply, or none
    def mark
      unless @inventory.fetch?(STAMP)
        DRC.message("Remedy: could not get the stamp; the #{@noun} is left unmarked.")
        return :no_stamp
      end

      reply = DRC.bput("mark my #{@noun} with my stamp", *Patterns::MARK_REPLIES)
      @inventory.stow_tool(STAMP)
      Patterns::STAMP_DAMAGED.match?(reply) ? :damaged : :sent
    end

    # Says the stamp needs repair and the product is left unmarked, and how
    # to have remedy repair it (remedy_repair_stamp).
    #
    # @return [void]
    def report_damaged
      DRC.message("Remedy: the stamp is too badly damaged to mark the #{@noun}; the #{@noun} is left unmarked. Repair the stamp, or set remedy_repair_stamp: true for remedy to repair it and mark again.")
    end

    # Counts the product in hand and prints its yield: "Remedy: made 12 uses
    # of clubmoss liniment." for "You count out 12 uses remaining."
    # (25:11333). The product is named as the hand holding it shows it
    # ({Inventory#held_name}), or by its noun when no hand shows it.
    #
    # The mark leaves roundtime ("Roundtime: 21 sec.", 25:11296 after the
    # mark at 25:11294), so the count waits it out first; sent in roundtime
    # the game would answer "...wait" instead of the count.
    #
    # A count with no number in its reply (no reply within bput's timeout,
    # or "I could not find what you were referring to.") still ends in
    # success: the product was checked in hand after its get ({#taken?}),
    # so the message is printed without the number and nothing stops.
    #
    # @return [void]
    def report_yield
      waitrt?
      uses = Remedy.counted("count my #{@noun}", Patterns::PRODUCT_COUNT_REPLIES)
      product = @inventory.held_name(@noun) || @noun
      DRC.message(uses ? "Remedy: made #{uses} uses of #{product}." : "Remedy: made #{product}; its uses could not be counted.")
    end
  end

  # Repairs a stamp the game called too badly damaged to mark the product,
  # when remedy_repair_stamp is set (opt-in, off by
  # default), so the {Finisher} can mark once more.
  #
  # The repair is DRCC.repair_own_tools with the character's own wire brush
  # and oil, as workorders.lic:385-389 runs it for workorders_repair_own_tools:
  # coins on hand first (DRCM.ensure_copper_on_hand, {COPPER}), a walk back
  # to the room, then the repair with the crafting town's base-crafting.yaml
  # blacksmithing data. The helper buys oil or a wire brush at that town's
  # finisher shop when the crafting container has too few uses, and walks
  # back (lich-5 common-crafting.rb, check_consumables).
  #
  # The helper holds the stamp in one hand and the brush or the oil in the
  # other, so the product cannot stay in hand: it is put in the crafting
  # container by its game id for the repair and got back by the same id,
  # so no other item of the same name is taken in its place. A product with
  # no id is not put away, and the stamp is not repaired.
  #
  # Should the script end during the repair (a commons helper's exit, a
  # ;kill), {#recover}, run from the before_dying cleanup, gets the product
  # back.
  #
  # @api private
  class StampRepair
    # The coins, in copper, made sure of on hand before the repair, as
    # workorders.lic:387 does before the same helper.
    COPPER = 1500

    # @param settings [OpenStruct] the character's settings; reads
    #   force_crafting_town and hometown (DRCC.crafting_hometown), and the
    #   bank helper reads more
    # @param inventory [Inventory] the product's put and get, and the repair
    # @param blacksmithing [Hash{String => Hash}, nil] base-crafting.yaml's
    #   blacksmithing data, by town
    def initialize(settings:, inventory:, blacksmithing:)
      @settings = settings
      @inventory = inventory
      @blacksmithing = blacksmithing || {}
      @aside = nil
    end

    # Repairs the stamp, the stamp put away and the product in hand (see the
    # class comment). Says why and repairs nothing when base-crafting.yaml
    # has no blacksmithing data for the crafting town, when the product in
    # hand shows no game id, or when it does not leave the hand.
    #
    # @param noun [String] the product noun
    # @return [Boolean] true when the repair ran and the product is back in
    #   hand, so the stamp may mark it once more; false when nothing was
    #   repaired and the product is still in hand
    # @raise [SystemExit] when the product cannot be got back after the
    #   repair ({Remedy.stop}), or when DRCC.repair_own_tools stops the
    #   script (its gets of the wire brush and the oil pass no skip_exit)
    def repair(noun)
      town = DRCC.crafting_hometown(@settings)
      info = @blacksmithing[town]
      return unrepaired(noun, "base-crafting.yaml has no blacksmithing data for #{town.inspect} to repair it with") unless info

      id = @inventory.held_id(noun)
      return unrepaired(noun, "the #{noun} in hand shows no id to put it away by during the repair") unless id

      DRC.message("Remedy: the stamp is too badly damaged to mark the #{noun}; repairing it, with the #{noun} in your #{@inventory.bag} meanwhile.")
      return unrepaired(noun, "the #{noun} could not be put in your #{@inventory.bag} to free both hands for the repair") unless @inventory.put_aside(id, noun)

      @aside = [id, noun]
      repair_stamp(town, info)
      Remedy.stop(lost(id, noun)) unless take_back
      true
    end

    # Gets the product back when the script ends while it is put away for
    # the repair; the before_dying cleanup runs it. Sends nothing otherwise.
    #
    # @return [void]
    def recover
      return unless @aside

      id, noun = @aside
      DRC.message("Remedy: #{lost(id, noun)}") unless take_back
    end

    private

    # Makes sure of coins on hand, walks back to the room, and repairs the
    # stamp ({Inventory#repair_tools}).
    #
    # @param town [String] the crafting town
    # @param info [Hash] its blacksmithing data
    # @return [void]
    def repair_stamp(town, info)
      room = Room.current&.id
      DRCM.ensure_copper_on_hand(COPPER, @settings, town)
      DRCT.walk_to(room) if room
      @inventory.repair_tools(info, [Finisher::STAMP])
    end

    # Gets the put-away product back by its id ({Inventory#take_back}).
    # Whatever the answer, it is not tried again.
    #
    # @return [Boolean] whether the product is in hand
    def take_back
      id, noun = @aside
      @aside = nil
      @inventory.take_back(id, noun)
    end

    # Says why the stamp is not repaired and the product is left unmarked.
    #
    # @param noun [String] the product noun
    # @param why [String] the reason, as a clause
    # @return [false]
    def unrepaired(noun, why)
      DRC.message("Remedy: the stamp is too badly damaged to mark the #{noun}, and #{why}; the #{noun} is left unmarked.")
      false
    end

    # Where the product is when it could not be got back.
    #
    # @param id [String] its game id
    # @param noun [String] the product noun
    # @return [String] a sentence
    def lost(id, noun)
      "the #{noun} is in your #{@inventory.bag} (##{id}): it could not be got back after the stamp repair."
    end
  end

  # The crafting magic: the commons' routine each crafting pass, and the
  # release at the end, or when remedy stops.
  #
  # @api private
  class Magic
    # The commands that let go of what the routine left prepared, in order,
    # each with the replies {#release} waits for.
    RELEASES = {
      'release spell' => Patterns::RELEASE_SPELL_REPLIES,
      'release mana'  => Patterns::RELEASE_MANA_REPLIES,
      'release symb'  => Patterns::RELEASE_SYMBIOSIS_REPLIES
    }.freeze

    # @param settings [OpenStruct] the character's settings; the routine
    #   reads them, the release reads crafting_training_spells
    def initialize(settings)
      @settings = settings
      @training_spells = settings.crafting_training_spells
      @ran = false
      @released = false
    end

    # Runs DRCA.crafting_magic_routine.
    #
    # @return [void]
    def routine
      @ran = true
      DRCA.crafting_magic_routine(@settings)
    end

    # Releases the spell, mana and symbiosis the routine left prepared, when
    # crafting_training_spells is set; remedy runs it once the product is
    # out.
    #
    # @return [void]
    # @note Unchanged from before: the setting's nil default answers
    #   `empty?` with nil under Lich (its NilClass#method_missing, lich-5
    #   lib/common/class_exts/nilclass.rb:9-11), so a run that
    #   finishes with the setting unset still sends the releases.
    def release
      return if @training_spells.empty?

      @released = true
      RELEASES.each { |command, replies| DRC.bput(command, *replies) }
    end

    # Releases the spell, mana and symbiosis from the before_dying cleanup,
    # when crafting_training_spells lists a spell, {#routine} has run (so
    # something may be prepared) and {#release} has not: a stop, or a
    # commons helper's exit, would otherwise leave them prepared
    # (H15).
    #
    # Sends with fput, which waits for no particular reply, as the
    # before_dying blocks of magic-training.lic:122-124 and
    # combat-trainer.lic:7092 do. Sends nothing when the setting is unset
    # (nil, its profiles/base.yaml default) or empty.
    #
    # @return [void]
    def release_on_exit
      return if !@ran || @released || Array(@training_spells).empty?

      @released = true
      RELEASES.each_key { |command| fput(command) }
    end
  end

  # A local copy of lich-5's DRCC.study_instructions, for the instructions
  # form ({Order#instructions?}): gets crafting instructions, studies them
  # until the game is ready to craft, and puts them away again unless the
  # game tossed them.
  #
  # It mirrors the helper proposed in elanthia-online/lich-5#2246
  # (common-crafting.rb, its
  # STUDY_INSTRUCTIONS_* constants, StudyInstructionsResult and
  # study_instructions with its private helpers): the same signature,
  # result, constants, steps and messages, with two differences:
  # - the GET's success is read from the hands, DRCI.in_hands?(name), not
  #   from DRCC.get_crafting_item's answer, which is nil on success too on
  #   lich-5 v5.20.0 to v5.22.0 (common-crafting.rb:324-363 at those tags);
  #   only v5.23.0 and later answer true or false. DRCI.in_hands? exists on
  #   every release and matches the hand's full name by the short_regex
  #   (/\bliniment.*\binstructions/i finds "shining clubmoss liniment
  #   instructions", 25:10795);
  # - its Flag has its own name ({STUDY_INSTRUCTIONS_DISCARDED_FLAG}), so it
  #   cannot collide with the helper's once both exist, and the failure
  #   report is its own ({.instructions_failed}), because
  #   DRCC.crafting_item_failed is private in lich-5.
  #
  # Remedy calls it once, from {Recipe::Instructions#prepare}, with skip_exit
  # true, and decides itself what a failure means.
  #
  # @deprecated Use DRCC.study_instructions once a lich-5 release ships it.
  # @note Remove once a lich-5 release ships DRCC.study_instructions
  #   (elanthia-online/lich-5#2246): the
  #   follow-up `refactor(remedy): use DRCC.study_instructions` deletes this
  #   module and its specs, calls DRCC.study_instructions from
  #   {Recipe::Instructions#prepare}, drops the Flag from
  #   {Remedy.delete_flags}, and adds a study_instructions default to
  #   Harness::DRCC in test/test_harness.rb. Until then, a change to the
  #   helper must be made here too; the API spec in spec/remedy_spec.rb
  #   pins the parameters and the result members.
  # @api private
  module LocalStudyInstructions
    # The first study of instructions that wear out asks for a second one,
    # on one line that also gives the uses left before this study:
    #   [These instructions have only 5 uses remaining.  Study them again to proceed.]
    # (25:10812). The uses part is optional so that a bare "Study them
    # again" prompt (what forge, sew, shape and tinker wait for) still
    # counts as a prompt. The game says "1 uses" for the last use
    # (25:30340); the singular "use" is accepted but has not been seen.
    # DRC.bput returns the matched text, from which +uses+ is read with
    # this same pattern.
    STUDY_INSTRUCTIONS_AGAIN = /(?:These instructions have only (?<uses>\d+) uses? remaining\.\s+)?Study them again/

    # Ends a completed study: "Roundtime: 7 sec." (25:10825).
    STUDY_INSTRUCTIONS_SUCCESS = /^Roundtime/

    # The reply to a `<verb> my <name>` that names nothing on the
    # character, as the GET of missing instructions got at 26:60708.
    #
    # @note code-derived (unverified) for a study: never seen after one.
    STUDY_INSTRUCTIONS_NOT_FOUND = /^What were you referring to/

    # A study when the game does not see the instructions in hand. "You must
    # be holding the instructions to study them." is the real reply to
    # `study instruc` in a 2026-11 enchanting log (enchant.lic matches it
    # too). "But you're not holding" is the reply to a book command when the
    # book is not in hand (25:10805).
    #
    # @note "But you're not holding" is code-derived (unverified) for a
    #   study: never seen after one.
    STUDY_INSTRUCTIONS_NOT_HOLDING = /^(?:But you're not holding|You must be holding the instructions)/

    # Sent after the Roundtime of the study that used the last use, so
    # DRC.bput has already returned when it arrives and a Flag has to catch
    # it (25:30349 then 25:30352; same order at 25:32936, 26:49540 and
    # 26:58546). Anchored like DRParser's spoof-guarded patterns (StatLine,
    # TDPValue): only tags or whitespace may come before the text, so
    # another player's speech or ESP that quotes it does not count as a
    # toss and skip the stow of instructions still in hand.
    STUDY_INSTRUCTIONS_DISCARDED = /^(?:<[^>]*>|\s)*The instructions have grown so smudged and faded from use that you can no longer make them out/

    # Flag that catches {STUDY_INSTRUCTIONS_DISCARDED} during one
    # {.study_instructions} call. Its own name, not the helper's
    # 'drcc-study-instructions-discarded', and a remedy- name, so
    # {Remedy.delete_flags} deletes it after a ;kill.
    STUDY_INSTRUCTIONS_DISCARDED_FLAG = 'remedy-instructions-discarded'

    # The uses count in the prompt before the study that uses the last use.
    # Every "1 uses" prompt in the logs is followed by the toss once that
    # study completes (25:30340 then 25:30352, 25:32923 then 25:32936;
    # 26:49528 then 26:49540, 26:58534 then 26:58546), and no set is tossed
    # without one. A completed study after this count is a toss even when
    # the Flag missed the line, so the instructions are not put away after
    # they are gone.
    STUDY_INSTRUCTIONS_LAST_USE = 1

    # Most studies sent per {.study_instructions} call. The game asks for
    # one more study, once (31 prompts in 62 studies over both logs); the
    # third is headroom, not an expected path.
    STUDY_INSTRUCTIONS_MAX_STUDIES = 3

    # The line {.instructions_failed} prints before it stops the script,
    # as DRCC.crafting_item_failed does (lich-5 common-crafting.rb:538 at
    # upstream main bae103f5).
    STUDY_INSTRUCTIONS_STOP_MESSAGE = 'DRCC: Cannot continue crafting without required item. Stopping script.'

    # What one {.study_instructions} call did.
    #
    # @!attribute [r] studied
    #   @return [Boolean] a study reply matched {STUDY_INSTRUCTIONS_SUCCESS}
    # @!attribute [r] uses_left
    #   @return [Integer, nil] 0 when the instructions were tossed; the
    #     game's count less one after a completed study (the count is shown
    #     before the study uses one: 5 at 25:10812, 4 on the next set at
    #     25:11657, and "1 uses" is followed by the toss at 25:30340/30352);
    #     the game's count when the study did not complete; nil when the
    #     game gave no count (instructions that do not wear out, or a
    #     failure first)
    # @!attribute [r] discarded
    #   @return [Boolean] true when the game tossed the worn-out
    #     instructions away after the study: the Flag caught the toss line,
    #     or the study that completed followed a "1 uses" prompt
    #     ({STUDY_INSTRUCTIONS_LAST_USE})
    #
    # There is no stow field: DRCC.stow_crafting_item answers true for a PUT
    # that timed out or got "What were you referring to?" (25:30361), so it
    # cannot tell whether the instructions were put away.
    #
    # Defined as a constant and reopened below without a superclass, as
    # {Order} is: Lich evals the whole script again on every run in the
    # same session and keeps its constants.
    StudyInstructionsResult = Data.define(:studied, :uses_left, :discarded)

    class StudyInstructionsResult
      # @return [Boolean] see #studied
      def studied?
        studied
      end

      # @return [Boolean] see #discarded
      def discarded?
        discarded
      end
    end

    module_function

    # Gets crafting instructions, studies them until the game is ready to
    # craft, and puts them away again unless the game tossed them.
    #
    # Steps:
    # 1. DRCC.get_crafting_item(+name+, +bag+, +bag_items+, +belt+,
    #    +skip_exit+); the instructions are got when a hand holds them
    #    afterwards (DRCI.in_hands?).
    # 2. `study my <name>`, by full name so a second set of instructions or
    #    another "instructions" item cannot answer it. A "Study them again"
    #    prompt sends it again, up to {STUDY_INSTRUCTIONS_MAX_STUDIES}
    #    studies in all; the prompt's uses count is kept for +uses_left+.
    # 3. Waits out the study's roundtime, then checks a Flag for
    #    {STUDY_INSTRUCTIONS_DISCARDED}, which arrives after the Roundtime
    #    line DRC.bput returned on. A completed study after a "1 uses"
    #    prompt counts as a toss even if the Flag missed the line.
    # 4. DRCC.stow_crafting_item(+name+, +bag+, +belt+), unless the
    #    instructions were tossed. A study that says they are not there or
    #    not in hand still comes after a GET that got them, so they are put
    #    away too; if they really are gone, the PUT only gets "What were you
    #    referring to?" (25:30361).
    #
    # A study that does not end in Roundtime (not found, not holding, a
    # timeout, or still "Study them again" after the last study) is a final
    # failure: it beeps, prints a bold line and stops the script, or returns
    # with +studied+ false when +skip_exit+ is set. The instructions are put
    # away first, so a stop does not leave them in hand. A GET that leaves
    # no instructions in hand returns +studied+ false without a message of
    # its own (DRCC.get_crafting_item has reported a final failure);
    # without +skip_exit+ it stops the script, since crafting cannot start
    # without the design.
    #
    # @param name [String] the instructions, as `get my`/`study my` take
    #   them, e.g. "liniment instructions"
    # @param bag [String] container that holds +bag_items+ and takes the
    #   instructions back
    # @param bag_items [Array<String>, nil] items kept in +bag+
    # @param belt [Hash, nil] crafting belt settings ('name', 'items')
    # @param skip_exit [Boolean] return a result on a final failure instead
    #   of stopping the script
    # @return [StudyInstructionsResult]
    # @raise [ArgumentError] when +name+ is nil or blank, before any command
    #   is sent (a blank name would GET or untie nothing in particular)
    # @raise [SystemExit] on a final failure without +skip_exit+, or when
    #   DRCC.get_crafting_item stops the script itself
    # @note When the GET's reply comes after DRC.bput gave up, the hands are
    #   read before the instructions arrive, and the call fails as if they
    #   were missing; the helper on v5.23.0 and later has the same window
    #   for that reply. No get in the logs timed out.
    #
    # @example Prepare a remedy from instructions
    #   result = LocalStudyInstructions.study_instructions('liniment instructions', bag, bag_items, belt, true)
    #   DRC.message('Last use of these instructions.') if result.discarded?
    def study_instructions(name, bag, bag_items, belt, skip_exit = false)
      raise ArgumentError, 'study_instructions needs the name of the instructions' if name.to_s.strip.empty?

      DRCC.get_crafting_item(name, bag, bag_items, belt, skip_exit)
      unless DRCI.in_hands?(name)
        # A final GET failure has already reported; only stop here.
        instructions_failed("DRCC: Could not get #{name} to study.", skip_exit) unless skip_exit
        return StudyInstructionsResult.new(studied: false, uses_left: nil, discarded: false)
      end

      outcome, uses, discarded = study_held_instructions(name)
      studied = outcome == :studied
      DRCC.stow_crafting_item(name, bag, belt) unless discarded
      instructions_failed(study_instructions_failure(outcome, name), skip_exit) unless studied
      StudyInstructionsResult.new(
        studied: studied,
        uses_left: discarded ? 0 : instructions_uses_left(uses, studied),
        discarded: discarded
      )
    end

    # Sends `study my <name>` until a reply other than "Study them again",
    # or {STUDY_INSTRUCTIONS_MAX_STUDIES} studies, then waits out roundtime
    # and reads the discard Flag. The Flag is added before the first study
    # and deleted on the way out, however the method leaves. A completed
    # study after a {STUDY_INSTRUCTIONS_LAST_USE} count is a toss too, so a
    # toss line that arrives after waitrt? cannot lead to a stow of
    # instructions that are gone.
    #
    # @param name [String] the instructions, as study_instructions got them
    # @return [Array(Symbol, Integer, Boolean)] the outcome (:studied,
    #   :again, :not_found, :not_holding or :timeout), the last uses count
    #   the game gave (nil if none), and whether the instructions were
    #   tossed
    def study_held_instructions(name)
      Flags.add(STUDY_INSTRUCTIONS_DISCARDED_FLAG, STUDY_INSTRUCTIONS_DISCARDED)
      outcome = :again
      uses = nil
      STUDY_INSTRUCTIONS_MAX_STUDIES.times do
        reply = DRC.bput("study my #{name}", STUDY_INSTRUCTIONS_AGAIN, STUDY_INSTRUCTIONS_SUCCESS, STUDY_INSTRUCTIONS_NOT_FOUND, STUDY_INSTRUCTIONS_NOT_HOLDING)
        outcome = study_instructions_outcome(reply)
        break unless outcome == :again

        uses = reply[STUDY_INSTRUCTIONS_AGAIN, :uses]&.to_i || uses
      end
      waitrt?
      tossed = Flags[STUDY_INSTRUCTIONS_DISCARDED_FLAG] || (outcome == :studied && uses == STUDY_INSTRUCTIONS_LAST_USE)
      [outcome, uses, tossed ? true : false]
    ensure
      Flags.delete(STUDY_INSTRUCTIONS_DISCARDED_FLAG)
    end

    # Classifies one study reply.
    #
    # @param reply [String] text DRC.bput matched ('' on a timeout)
    # @return [Symbol] :again, :studied, :not_found, :not_holding or :timeout
    def study_instructions_outcome(reply)
      case reply
      when STUDY_INSTRUCTIONS_AGAIN then :again
      when STUDY_INSTRUCTIONS_SUCCESS then :studied
      when STUDY_INSTRUCTIONS_NOT_FOUND then :not_found
      when STUDY_INSTRUCTIONS_NOT_HOLDING then :not_holding
      else :timeout
      end
    end

    # The bold line for a study that did not end in Roundtime.
    #
    # @param outcome [Symbol] a study_instructions_outcome other than :studied
    # @param name [String] the instructions
    # @return [String]
    def study_instructions_failure(outcome, name)
      case outcome
      when :again then "DRCC: Still told to study #{name} again after #{STUDY_INSTRUCTIONS_MAX_STUDIES} studies."
      when :not_found then "DRCC: Could not find #{name} to study."
      when :not_holding then "DRCC: Not holding #{name} to study."
      else "DRCC: No reply to studying #{name}."
      end
    end

    # Uses left after a study_instructions call that kept the instructions.
    #
    # @param uses [Integer, nil] the game's count, taken before the study
    # @param studied [Boolean] whether the study completed (and so used one)
    # @return [Integer, nil] nil when the game gave no count
    def instructions_uses_left(uses, studied)
      return nil unless uses

      studied ? uses - 1 : uses
    end

    # Reports a final failure as DRCC.crafting_item_failed does (lich-5
    # common-crafting.rb:533-540 at upstream main bae103f5, private there): beeps and
    # prints +message+ in bold, then, unless +skip_exit+, prints
    # {STUDY_INSTRUCTIONS_STOP_MESSAGE} and stops the script. DRC.message is
    # the bold line on every lich-5 release.
    #
    # @param message [String] bold line naming the failure
    # @param skip_exit [Boolean] return instead of stopping the script
    # @return [false] when +skip_exit+
    # @raise [SystemExit] without +skip_exit+
    def instructions_failed(message, skip_exit)
      DRC.beep
      DRC.message(message)
      return false if skip_exit

      DRC.message(STUDY_INSTRUCTIONS_STOP_MESSAGE)
      exit
    end

    # Internal to study_instructions; kept off the module's public API, as
    # the helper keeps them off DRCC's.
    private_class_method :study_held_instructions, :study_instructions_outcome,
                         :study_instructions_failure, :instructions_uses_left,
                         :instructions_failed
  end

  # Where a fresh craft (the book or the instructions form) studies its
  # recipe before the herb goes in: one strategy class per recipe source,
  # each with {Book#prepare} and {Book#studied_instructions}, picked by the
  # order's form ({.for}). A new recipe source is a new class and a new
  # {SOURCES} entry; {Remedy#create_item} only calls #prepare.
  #
  # The continue form has no entry: it studies nothing, and resumes the
  # remedy in the vessel without the stows, cube, flower swap and herb
  # gathering a fresh craft does ({Remedy#run}).
  #
  # @api private
  module Recipe
    # Finds the recipe in a book: master_crafting_book when it is set,
    # otherwise the remed book.
    #
    # Without master_crafting_book, the remed book is got with a guarded get
    # ({Inventory#fetch!}) and stowed, both with alchemy_belt like every
    # other tool. remedy used to pass no belt for the book (H08: it
    # read a @forging_belt that was never set), so DRCC.get_crafting_item
    # never untied it from alchemy_belt and DRCC.stow_crafting_item put it in
    # the crafting container even when alchemy_belt lists it. A book that
    # cannot be got stops remedy with "Remedy: could not get remed book.
    # Stopping." on every lich-5 release. The warning that the remed book is
    # outgrown ({BOOK_WARNING}) is printed from {BOOK_WARNING_RANK} Alchemy
    # ranks up.
    #
    # @api private
    class Book
      # @param order [Order] a book-form order (chapter, recipe name, book
      #   type)
      # @param settings [OpenStruct] the character's settings; reads
      #   master_crafting_book
      # @param inventory [Inventory] gets and stows the remed book
      def initialize(order:, settings:, inventory:)
        @order = order
        @settings = settings
        @inventory = inventory
      end

      # Turns the book to the recipe's page (DRCC.find_recipe2).
      #
      # @return [void]
      # @raise [SystemExit] when the remed book cannot be got, or when a
      #   commons helper stops the script
      def prepare
        if @settings.master_crafting_book
          DRCC.find_recipe2(@order.chapter, @order.recipe_name, @settings.master_crafting_book, @order.book_type)
        else
          @inventory.fetch!(REMED_BOOK)
          echo(BOOK_WARNING) if DRSkill.getrank('Alchemy') >= BOOK_WARNING_RANK
          DRCC.find_recipe2(@order.chapter, @order.recipe_name)
          @inventory.stow_tool(REMED_BOOK)
        end
      end

      # What a stop for faded instructions calls the instructions studied,
      # as remedy has always said it.
      #
      # @return [String] e.g. "the instructions for some head unguent"
      def studied_instructions
        "the instructions for #{@order.recipe_name}"
      end
    end

    # Studies loose crafting instructions, "<noun> instructions"
    # ({Order#instructions_name}), with the local study_instructions
    # ({LocalStudyInstructions.study_instructions}), routed like every
    # other crafting item: from crafting_container (by name when
    # crafting_items_in_container lists them) or alchemy_belt, and back
    # there. No book is opened.
    #
    # @api private
    class Instructions
      # @param order [Order] an instructions-form order
      # @param inventory [Inventory] the crafting container, its listed
      #   items and alchemy_belt the instructions are got from
      # @param _others [Hash] the other keywords {Recipe.for} passes every
      #   source (settings), which instructions do not read
      def initialize(order:, inventory:, **_others)
        @order = order
        @inventory = inventory
      end

      # Studies the instructions with skip_exit, so remedy decides what
      # each result means:
      # - not studied (the instructions cannot be got, or the study never
      #   ends in Roundtime): stops with "Remedy: could not study the <noun>
      #   instructions. Stopping.", after the helper put any instructions
      #   in hand away, before any herb is gathered;
      # - tossed (the study used their last use): says so, then crafts on;
      # - a uses count the game gave: says how many uses are left, then
      #   crafts on.
      #
      # @return [void]
      # @raise [SystemExit] when the instructions are not studied, or when
      #   a commons helper stops the script
      def prepare
        name = @order.instructions_name
        result = LocalStudyInstructions.study_instructions(name, @inventory.bag, @inventory.bag_items, @inventory.belt, true)
        Remedy.stop("could not study the #{name}.") unless result.studied?
        if result.discarded?
          DRC.message("Remedy: that was the last use of the #{name}; they are gone.")
        elsif result.uses_left
          DRC.message("Remedy: #{result.uses_left} #{result.uses_left == 1 ? 'use' : 'uses'} left on the #{name}.")
        end
      end

      # What a stop for faded instructions calls the instructions studied;
      # the instructions form has no recipe name.
      #
      # @return [String] e.g. "the salve instructions"
      def studied_instructions
        "the #{@order.instructions_name}"
      end
    end

    # The recipe source of each form that studies a recipe, by
    # {Order#mode}.
    SOURCES = { book: Book, instructions: Instructions }.freeze

    # The recipe source for an order.
    #
    # @param order [Order] a book- or instructions-form order
    # @param settings [OpenStruct] the character's settings
    # @param inventory [Inventory] where the book or instructions live
    # @return [Book, Instructions]
    # @raise [KeyError] for a continue-form order, which studies nothing
    def self.for(order, settings:, inventory:)
      SOURCES.fetch(order.mode).new(order: order, settings: settings, inventory: inventory)
    end
  end

  # Deletes every remedy Flag: {REGISTERED_FLAGS}, and the Flag the local
  # study_instructions adds while it studies
  # ({LocalStudyInstructions::STUDY_INSTRUCTIONS_DISCARDED_FLAG}), which it
  # deletes itself unless the script is killed mid-study. The before_dying
  # cleanup {#run} registers ends with it, so it runs however the script
  # ends.
  #
  # @return [void]
  def self.delete_flags
    REGISTERED_FLAGS.each_key { |name| Flags.delete(name) }
    Flags.delete(LocalStudyInstructions::STUDY_INSTRUCTIONS_DISCARDED_FLAG)
  end

  # Stops the script: beeps, prints a bold "Remedy: <why> Stopping." line
  # and exits. The vessel stays in hand, with any unfinished remedy in it;
  # the before_dying cleanup ({#run}) then puts the stirrer away, releases
  # the crafting magic and, when the remedy can be carried on, prints the
  # `;remedy continue` line. The collaborators stop through it too, so
  # every stop reads the same.
  #
  # @param message [String] why remedy stops, as a sentence
  # @return [void] never returns
  # @raise [SystemExit] always
  def self.stop(message)
    DRC.beep
    DRC.message("Remedy: #{message} Stopping.")
    exit
  end

  # Sends a count and reads the number its reply starts with: "You count
  # out 75 pieces of material there." (25:8678, a herb) or "You count out
  # 12 uses remaining." (25:11333, a remedy). The herb gatherer and the
  # finisher count through it.
  #
  # @param command [String] the count, e.g. `count my nemoih`
  # @param patterns [Array<Regexp>] its replies, for DRC.bput
  # @return [Integer, nil] the number, or nil when the reply holds none (no
  #   reply within bput's timeout, or "I could not find what you were
  #   referring to.", 25:13762)
  def self.counted(command, patterns)
    DRC.bput(command, *patterns)[/\d+/]&.to_i
  end

  # Takes the settings and the parsed arguments. Sends nothing to the game.
  #
  # @param settings [OpenStruct] the character's settings (get_settings)
  # @param args [OpenStruct] the parsed arguments, in one of
  #   {ARG_DEFINITIONS}' forms
  def initialize(settings, args)
    @settings = settings
    @cube = settings.cube_armor_piece
    @magic = Magic.new(settings)
    @order = Order.from_args(args)
    @inventory = Inventory.new(settings, herbs: @order.herbs)
    @tools = nil
    @craft_loop = nil
    @blacksmithing = nil
    @stamp_repair = nil
    @recipe = nil
    @finished = false
  end

  # Crafts the remedy, from preparing the supplies through to the product in
  # hand, then exits. Registers {#cleanup} with before_dying first, so it
  # runs however the script ends: a finish, a stop, a commons helper's exit
  # or a ;kill.
  #
  # @return [void]
  # @raise [SystemExit] when the remedy is finished, or when a check or a
  #   commons helper stops the script
  def run
    before_dying { cleanup }
    # The room remedy was started in is read before anything moves the
    # character, so a purchase can walk back to it.
    crafting = get_data('crafting')
    @blacksmithing = crafting['blacksmithing']
    supplies = Supplies.new(settings: @settings, remedies: crafting['remedies'], inventory: @inventory,
                            return_room: @settings.alchemy_room || Room.current&.id)
    REGISTERED_FLAGS.each { |name, patterns| Flags.add(name, *patterns) }

    @tools = Tools.resolve(@settings, @order)

    @water = Liquid.find('water', @settings.water_container, @inventory)
    @alcohol = Liquid.find('alcohol', @settings.alcohol_container, @inventory)
    supplies.buy_missing(water: @water, alcohol: @alcohol, catalyst: @order.catalyst)
    DRC.wait_for_script_to_complete('buff', ['remedy']) if buff_set?

    if @order.continue?
      @inventory.fetch!(@tools.stirrer)
      craft("analyze my #{@order.noun}")
    else
      create_item
    end
  end

  private

  # Whether ;buff remedy may cast anything: false only when buff.lic would
  # print "No waggle set found for name: remedy" and exit at once
  # (buff.lic:37-42, `unless settings.waggle_sets[setname]`), which is when
  # waggle_sets is unset (nil, its profiles/base.yaml default; Lich answers
  # nil['remedy'] with nil, lich-5 lib/common/class_exts/nilclass.rb:9-11)
  # or is a Hash with no truthy 'remedy' entry. Any other
  # waggle_sets value runs ;buff remedy as before, whatever buff.lic then
  # makes of it. An empty remedy set ({}) is truthy, so
  # buff.lic would go on to DRCA.do_buffs, and ;buff remedy runs.
  #
  # @return [Boolean]
  # @note Reads the settings remedy was started with, the same get_settings
  #   buff.lic calls. A custom buff.lic that does something without a remedy
  #   waggle set is not run either.
  def buff_set?
    sets = @settings.waggle_sets
    return false if sets.nil?
    return true unless sets.is_a?(Hash)

    sets['remedy'] ? true : false
  end

  # Leaves things tidy however the script ended. After a stop (the run did
  # not finish), gets back the product a stamp repair put away
  # ({StampRepair#recover}), puts the stirrer away when a hand holds it, so the vessel
  # stays in hand with any unfinished remedy, and prints
  # the `;remedy continue` line when the remedy can be carried on
  # ({CraftLoop#resumable?}). Then releases the crafting magic when
  # crafting_training_spells is set and the finish did not
  # ({Magic#release_on_exit}), and deletes the Flags whatever happened
  # before ({Remedy.delete_flags}).
  #
  # @return [void]
  # @note The stirrer's stow goes through DRCC.stow_crafting_item, which
  #   reads the reply with DRC.bput, as DRCA.release_cyclics does from the
  #   before_dying block of combat-trainer.lic:7088-7089. After a normal
  #   finish nothing is stowed: the stirrer is already away, and the product
  #   in hand is never touched.
  def cleanup
    unless @finished
      @stamp_repair&.recover
      @inventory.stow_held(@tools.stirrer) if @tools
      report_resumable
    end
    @magic.release_on_exit
  ensure
    Remedy.delete_flags
  end

  # Prints how to carry on the unfinished remedy in the vessel, when there
  # is one ({CraftLoop#resumable?}): "Remedy: the unfinished unguent is in
  # the large mortar; to carry on with it, run ;remedy continue na \"pewter
  # ingot\" mortar unguent".
  #
  # @return [void]
  def report_resumable
    return unless @craft_loop&.resumable?

    DRC.message("Remedy: the unfinished #{@order.noun} is in the #{@tools.container}; to carry on with it, run #{@order.continue_command}")
  end

  # Crafts with another herb1 than the one asked for (the dried or crushed
  # flower), and routes that name as a herb from now on.
  #
  # @param name [String] the herb to use
  # @return [void]
  def use_herb1(name)
    @order = @order.with(herb1: name)
    @inventory = @inventory.with_herbs(@order.herbs)
  end

  # Crafts with the prepared flower ({FLOWER_SUBSTITUTES}) when herb1 is a
  # fresh flower's name or a part of it, the first such flower in
  # {FLOWER_SUBSTITUTES}, and the character has none of that fresh flower
  # (DRCI.exists? of its whole name, a tap). This is remedy's old
  # 'red flower'.include?(herb1) rule, unchanged, so a typed red or blue
  # crafts with the right prepared flower. Any herb1 that is no part of a
  # flower name is crafted as given and nothing is sent.
  #
  # @return [void]
  # @note A part of a flower name other than flower is not a noun the game
  #   knows (DR matches a command noun by the start of the item's noun), so
  #   with the fresh flower on hand such a herb1 is kept as given and its get
  #   fails, as it always has.
  # @note herb1 flower is a part of both names, so it takes the red flower's
  #   dried flower, even for a blue-flower recipe (H10, left open).
  #   Gathering it as given would take whichever flower comes first in the
  #   container (workorders.lic:726-727), the wrong herb for a red-flower
  #   recipe when a crushed flower comes first, and remedy cannot tell
  #   which flower the recipe needs.
  def substitute_flower
    fresh, prepared = FLOWER_SUBSTITUTES.find { |name, _| name.include?(@order.herb1) }
    use_herb1(prepared) if fresh && !DRCI.exists?(fresh)
  end

  # Studies the recipe from its source ({Recipe.for}: the book, or in the
  # instructions form the crafting instructions), gathers the herb into the
  # vessel, then crafts.
  #
  # {FADED_FLAG} is reset once the recipe is studied, so only a fade of
  # these instructions can stop the crafting, not one of a study before.
  #
  # @return [void]
  # @raise [SystemExit] when the remedy is finished, or when remedy or a
  #   commons helper stops the script
  def create_item
    @magic.routine
    @inventory.stow(DRC.left_hand)
    @inventory.stow(DRC.right_hand)
    pause 0.2 # to prevent issues with lag during the stow commands
    @recipe = Recipe.for(@order, settings: @settings, inventory: @inventory)
    @recipe.prepare
    Flags.reset(FADED_FLAG)

    DRC.bput("touch my #{@cube}", *Patterns::CUBE_TOUCH_REPLIES) if @cube

    substitute_flower
    HerbGatherer.new(@inventory, minimum: @order.herb_quantity).gather(@order.herb1, @tools.container)
    @inventory.fetch!(@tools.stirrer)

    craft(@tools.first_command(@order.herb1), studied: true)
  end

  # Runs the crafting loop from the first command; when the remedy is done,
  # takes it out, releases magic and exits. When the game refuses the herb,
  # the instructions fade or the loop gives up, stops with a Remedy line
  # saying why.
  #
  # @param command [String] the first crafting command
  # @param studied [Boolean] the recipe was studied for this run (see
  #   {CraftLoop#run})
  # @return [void] never returns
  # @raise [SystemExit] when the remedy is finished, when remedy stops, or
  #   when a commons helper stops the script
  def craft(command, studied: false)
    handlers = Handlers.new(order: @order, tools: @tools, inventory: @inventory, water: @water, alcohol: @alcohol)
    @craft_loop = CraftLoop.new(tools: @tools, noun: @order.noun, handlers: handlers, magic: @magic)
    case @craft_loop.run(command, studied: studied)
    when :unsuitable_herb
      stop("the game wants a more suitable type of herb than #{herb_in_vessel}: it refused #{@craft_loop.command.inspect}.")
    when :more_pieces
      stop("the #{@order.noun} needs more pieces of #{herb_in_vessel} to begin: the game refused #{@craft_loop.command.inspect}.")
    when :faded
      stop("#{@recipe.studied_instructions} faded from memory before crafting began; run remedy again to study them.")
    when :unanswered
      stop("the game did not answer #{@craft_loop.command.inspect} #{CraftLoop::MAX_SILENT_REPLIES + 1} times in a row.")
    when :too_long
      stop("the #{@order.noun} is not done after #{CraftLoop::MAX_CRAFT_STEPS} crafting commands.")
    end

    @stamp_repair = StampRepair.new(settings: @settings, inventory: @inventory, blacksmithing: @blacksmithing) if @settings.remedy_repair_stamp
    Finisher.new(inventory: @inventory, tools: @tools, noun: @order.noun, mark: @settings.mark_crafted_goods, repair: @stamp_repair).finish
    @finished = true
    @magic.release
    exit
  end

  # The herb a refusal names: herb1, or in the continue form, which is not
  # told the herb, the herb in the vessel.
  #
  # @return [String]
  def herb_in_vessel
    @order.herb1 || "the herb in the #{@tools.container}"
  end

  # Stops the script ({Remedy.stop}).
  #
  # @param message [String] why remedy stops, as a sentence
  # @return [void] never returns
  # @raise [SystemExit] always
  def stop(message)
    Remedy.stop(message)
  end
end

args = parse_args(Remedy::ARG_DEFINITIONS)
Remedy.new(get_settings, args).run
